1. d4 f5 2. c4 Nf6 3. Nc3 g6 {Introducing the "Lenningrad" system.} (3... e6 {
leads to traditional Dutch defence positions.}) 4. Nf3 Bg7 5. e3 O-O 6. b3 d6
7. Bd3 c6 {Larsen usually employs this method.} 8. Bb2 Qc7 9. O-O Na6 {so far
I have been following Urbanek v Vesely Prague 1957} 10. Re1 e5 11. Nd2 e4 12.
Bf1 $1 Ng4 13. h3 Nh6 14. Ne2 Nb4 15. Ng3 d5 16. cxd5 cxd5 17. Rc1 Qd6 18. Ba3
{This pin does not achieve much.} f4 $1 19. Ne2 (19. exf4 {is very risky} Rxf4
20. Re2 Bxd4) 19... f3 $1 20. Nc3 (20. gxf3 {leaves the White King rather bare.
}) 20... fxg2 21. Bxg2 Rxf2 $3 22. Nxd5 (22. Kxf2 $2 Nd3+ 23. Kg1 Qxa3) 22...
Bxh3 $3 23. Re2 (23. Bxh3 Qg3+) (23. Kxf2 Rf8+ 24. Ke2 Qa6+) 23... Ng4 $1 24.
Rxf2 Qh2+ 25. Kf1 Nxd5 26. Qe2 Ngxe3+ 27. Ke1 Nxg2+ 28. Rxg2 Qxg2 29. Qxg2 Bxg2
{An excellent and interesting game. A fine KS attack} 0-1

