1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 g6 3. Nc3 Bg7 4. Nf3 d6 5. e3 {In view of his 8th a wasted move
} O-O 6. Be2 Nbd7 7. O-O e5 8. e4 Re8 {the threat is 9.......exd} 9. d5 Nc5 10.
Bg5 a5 {to prevent 11.b4} 11. Nd2 h6 12. Be3 Ra6 $2 {I thought this was a bad
move at the time but still played it in order to play ...c6 without getting
doubled Pawns. Which would result on playing it immediately as the following
variation shows.} (12... c6 13. Bxc5 dxc5 14. dxc6 bxc6) 13. f3 c6 14. b3 cxd5
15. cxd5 Ra8 16. Nc4 Rb8 {to play....b5} 17. Nb5 Bf8 {Things don't look too
good} 18. Qd2 $1 {wins a P} g5 19. Nxa5 Bd7 ({Not} 19... b6 $4 20. Nc6) 20. a4
$2 {weakens the b-Pawn} Ra8 $1 21. Nc4 $2 Nxb3 22. Qb4 Nxa1 23. Rxa1 Bxb5 24.
Qxb5 Qd7 $2 25. Nb6 Qxb5 26. Bxb5 {[Adjourned] The game resumed on 21st October
} Rac8 27. Bxe8 {better than 27.Nxc8 but giving up the Bishop pair} ({so that} 
27. Nxc8 Rxc8 {I have control of the c-file.}) 27... Rxe8 28. Rc1 Re7 {
prevents invasion on the 7th} 29. Nc8 Rd7 30. Bb6 Ne8 31. Na7 Be7 32. Nb5 Bd8
33. Bxd8 Rxd8 34. Nc7 $4 Rc8 {White had the better position at the adjournment
but badly misplayed the wholw ending} 0-1

