1. e4 d5 2. exd5 Nf6 3. Bb5+ {Usually played with the idea of holding onto the
gambit Pawn, at the cost of development.} c6 (3... Bd7 4. Bc4) 4. dxc6 bxc6 5.
Be2 {A similar move to that played by Bronstein in the 1960 Russian
Championship.} (5. Ba4 Ba6 {Is strong.}) (5. Bc4 Qd4 {Might prove a nuisance
later on.}) 5... e5 6. d3 Qd4 $2 {Waste of 4 tempi.} 7. Nf3 Qb4+ 8. c3 Qc5 9.
Be3 Qb5 {The 4th move with the Queen. The position of the Queen leaves it
vulnerable to d4.} 10. Qc1 {Protects the b-Pawn.} ({If} 10. Qc2 Bf5 {Is strong.
}) 10... Ng4 11. b3 {To free the Queen.} Nxe3 12. Qxe3 Bc5 $4 13. Qxe5+ Be6 $4
(13... Be7 $142) 14. d4 Nd7 15. Bxb5 Nxe5 16. Nxe5 cxb5 17. dxc5 O-O 18. O-O
Rfe8 {Threatening 19.....Bxb3} 19. Nf3 h6 20. Na3 Rab8 21. Nc2 Red8 22. Rfd1
Rdc8 23. b4 Bc4 24. Nfd4 Re8 25. Ne3 Be6 26. Nxe6 Rxe6 27. a3 {To play 28.c4}
f5 $2 28. Nxf5 Re2 29. Nd4 Rb2 30. Re1 Rf8 31. Re2 Rxe2 32. Nxe2 Rd8 33. Nd4 a6
34. a4 a5 35. Nxb5 axb4 36. cxb4 g5 37. Nd6 g4 38. a5 h5 39. a6 h4 40. a7 Ra8
41. Nb5 h3 42. g3 Kf7 43. Nc7 Rd8 44. a8=Q Rd4 45. Qe8+ Kf6 46. Ra6+ Kg5 47.
Qg6# {An easy game. Hayes won the match 6-2} 1-0

