{[Time limit 36 moves in 75 mins. Opponent Info: 7th in London U-18 Ch 60-61} 
1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 e6 3. Nc3 Bb4 4. e3 {The most popular method nowadays.} O-O 5.
a3 Bxc3+ {[2']} 6. bxc3 {[1']} c5 {[3']} 7. Nf3 {[1']} cxd4 {[5']} (7... d5 {
is more usual.}) 8. cxd4 {[1']} d5 {[5']} 9. Bd3 {[2']} dxc4 {[6'] My opening
has rsulted in my gaining a QS Pawn majority. I am weak on e5 but I can
develop easily.} 10. Bxc4 {[2']} a6 {[7']} 11. O-O {[10']} ({White says he
considered....} 11. a4 {...but I gain a tempo by....} Qc7) 11... b5 {[8']} 12.
Bd3 {[10']} (12. Ba2 {Might be better followed by an eventual d5.}) 12... Bb7 {
[8' 30s]} 13. Bd2 {[15']} Nc6 {Prevents 14.Ne5 & Bb4} 14. Rb1 {[14'] Preparing
a4.} Qe7 {[17']} 15. Qb3 {[21']} Rfc8 {[23'] Now I become cramped.} 16. a4 {
[23']} Na7 {[27']} 17. Ne5 {[32']} ({Now if} 17. axb5 axb5 18. Bxb5 $2 Nxb5 19.
Qxb5 Ba6 $1 {Wins the exchange.}) 17... Rcb8 {[30'] The position is rather
tricky. If I play 17.....Rab8 I can't play the previous variation.} 18. axb5 {
[42']} axb5 {[31']} 19. Bb4 {[43']} Qd8 {[33']} (19... Qe8 20. Bd6 $1) 20. Rfc1
{[50']} Be4 {[46'] I wasn't very happy at this stage and wanted to relieve the
pressure on my b-Pawn by exchanging off White's KBishop.} ({If....} 20... Qd5
21. Qb2 Z0 {followed by...} 22. Bc5 {winning the b-Pawn}) 21. Ra1 {[50']} Bxd3
{[56']} 22. Qxd3 {[50']} Rc8 {[60']} 23. Bc5 {[55'] This Bishop in reality
pinned.} Nd7 {[63'] Easing my position by exchanges.} 24. Nxd7 {[60']} Qxd7 {
[63' 30s]} 25. Qa3 $1 {[64']} h6 $1 {[65' 30s]} (25... Z0 {Intending...} 26.
Bxa7 Rxc1+ 27. Rxc1 Rxa7 28. Qxa7 Qxa7 $4 29. Rc8#) 26. h3 {[68'] Black also
has back-row mating threats in some variations. Here I offered a draw which
was accepted. (I intended otherwise 26.....Qb7) Middlesex lost the match 28 -
32. On March 8th I should have played Bd8 in the S.C.C.U. counties U-18
Championships but arrived late due to a train delay and a reserve was playing
in my place.} 1/2-1/2

