1. d4 {I rarely open with this nowadays.} g6 2. e4 Bg7 3. Nf3 d6 4. Bc4 {
I prefer this to 4.c4 as the Bishop exerts great pressure down the a2-g8
diagonal.} Nc6 5. Be3 e5 6. dxe5 (6. d5 {Is quite good but I didn't fancy it.})
6... Nxe5 (6... dxe5 7. Qxd8+ Nxd8 8. Nc3 Ne7 9. Nb5 {is not very good for
Black.} (9. Ng5)) 7. Bd4 {making it awkward for Black.} Bg4 (7... Nxc4 $2 8.
Bxg7 $18) (7... Nxf3+ 8. Qxf3 Nf6 9. Nc3 $16) 8. Bxe5 dxe5 (8... Bxe5 9. Bxf7+
Kxf7 (9... Ke7 10. c3) 10. Nxe5+ dxe5 11. Qxg4) 9. Nc3 Qxd1+ 10. Rxd1 c6 {
Preventing 11.Nd5 or Nb5} 11. h3 {The Bishop is a nuisance.} Bxf3 {Gives up
the two Bishops.} (11... Bd7 {Loses a Pawn.}) 12. gxf3 Nf6 13. O-O O-O 14. Rd6
{So as to double Rooks. The Rook is quite safe here.} Ne8 $2 (14... Nh5 15. Z0
Nf4 {Is a better plan.}) 15. Rd7 Rb8 {I refused an offered draw here.} 16. Rfd1
{Threatening 17.Rxf7} Bh6 $2 17. h4 {Prevents 17.....Bg5 but 17.Rxf7 is
correct.} (17. Rxf7 $142) 17... a5 $2 18. Rxf7 Rxf7 19. Rd7 Nd6 20. Rxd6 Kf8
21. Bxf7 Kxf7 22. Rd7+ {Securing the 7th.} Kg8 23. Ne2 Bg7 24. f4 {Giving up a
pawn for a positional advantage and a passed protected e-Pawn} exf4 25. Nxf4
Bxb2 26. h5 {Best.} g5 27. Ne6 {Threatening 28.Nd8} Bf6 28. h6 {Threatening 29.
Nxg5 The game was adjourned here. Play resumed 28/4/61} Re8 {[sealed move] I
offered a draw he accepted. Peter Adams and I were leading after 3 rounds with
3 each. After this round we had 3.5 as did Brian Gillman and we three led.
Next round Adams had to play Gillman.} ({Alternative sealed move.} 28... Kh8 $2
29. Rf7 Bc3 30. Nxg5 Be5 31. Rxh7+ Kg8 32. Rd7 $18) ({Alternative S.M} 28... g4
29. Ng5 Bxg5 30. Rg7+ Kf8 31. Rxg5 Rd8 32. Rxg4 Rd2 33. Rg7 Rxc2 34. Rxh7 Rxa2
35. Rxb7 Kg8 36. h7+ Kh8 37. Rb6 Rc2 38. Ra6 Rc5 39. Ra7 Re5 40. f3 Rb5 41. Ra6
c5 42. Kf2 $18) (28... Re8 29. Rd6 $2 (29. Nxg5 Bxg5 30. Rg7+ Kf8 31. Rxg5 Rxe4
32. Rg7 Re2 33. Rxb7 Rxc2 34. Rxh7 Rxa2 35. Kf1 $11) (29. Nc5 Re5 30. Nxb7 Rxe4
31. Nxa5 Re2 32. Nxc6 Rxc2 33. Nb4 Rb2 34. Nd5 Be5 $11) 29... Be5 30. Nc7 Rc8
$19) 1/2-1/2

