{Opponent Info: Grade 5a (177-184 ECF); Played in the 1957 British
Championship.} 1. d4 Nf6 2. e3 e6 3. Bd3 c5 4. c3 Nc6 5. f4 {A stonewall set
up.} b6 6. Nf3 Bb7 7. O-O Be7 {I don't want to play 7.....d5 as White will
presumably play Ne5 later after which I shall be able to answer ...d6} 8. Nbd2
{[3']} d6 {[15']} 9. e4 cxd4 10. cxd4 O-O 11. a3 {[6'] Preventing the QKnight
having much scope but it is quite happy on c6} Re8 {Allowing ...Bf8 if
necessary.} 12. e5 {This leaves some weaknesses in White's game namely his
d-Pawn.} Nd5 {Threatens e6 & the f-Pawn} 13. Ne4 dxe5 14. fxe5 {Now White is
very weak.} f5 {Forcing the issue.} 15. exf6 Nxf6 16. Neg5 Qd5 $1 {This
defends the e-Pawn, attacks the White d-pawn eyes the Knight on g5 and
threatens an eventual mate on g2.} 17. Qe2 {Defending g2 and attacking my
e-Pawn but not realising his d-Pawn will be lost for no compensation.} Nxd4 {
Also defends the e-Pawn} 18. Nxd4 Qxd4+ 19. Be3 {Playing for the attack.} (19.
Kh1 {Looks a bit better.}) 19... Qe5 {Now White is rather tied up.} 20. Rac1 {
Rather an unimportant open file.} Bc5 {Forcing simplification.} 21. Bxc5 Qxg5
22. Bd4 e5 {This Pawn proves quite strong.} 23. Be3 Qg4 24. Qd2 (24. Qxg4 {
Leads to a lost endgame.}) 24... Rad8 {Threatening 25.....RxB!!} 25. Rc3 {
Losing the exchange but he had little choice.} (25. Rf2 Ne4 $1) 25... Ne4 26.
Qc2 Nxc3 27. Bxh7+ Kh8 28. bxc3 Qxg2+ ({I also considered...} 28... g6 29. Bxg6
Rg8 {wins more easily.}) 29. Qxg2 Bxg2 30. Kxg2 Kxh7 31. Rf7 Rd3 32. Bf2 Rxc3
33. Rxa7 b5 {Placing the Pawn on a White square} 34. Be1 Rd3 35. Bb4 e4 36. Rb7
Rd5 37. Bc3 Rg5+ 38. Kf2 Rf8+ 39. Ke3 Rf3+ 40. Kd4 Rd3+ {Hayes won the Match
8-7 (15Bd)} 0-1

