1. c4 Nf6 2. g3 e6 3. Bg2 d5 4. d4 Be7 5. Nf3 Nbd7 6. b3 O-O 7. O-O dxc4 8.
bxc4 c5 9. e3 Nb6 10. Qd3 Qc7 11. Nbd2 Bd7 12. Ba3 {As a result of the opening
White has more space but Black's pieces have fairly good prospects. White also
controls the b-file.} Rfe8 13. Rfc1 Rad8 14. Nb3 {White manoeuvres on the QS
as Black has placed his Rook on d8 where it has little future and would have
been better reinforcing the QS.} cxd4 15. Bxe7 Rxe7 16. exd4 Bc6 {Black tries
to take advantage of White's d-pawn which is apparently weak. This move also
controls e4.} 17. c5 {This move apparently leaves the d-Pawn behind and gives
Black the d5 square for his Knight. However the d-Pawn is stronger than it
looks and Black finds that his Knight on d5 proves to be rather in the way.}
Nbd5 18. a3 {Now the Knight is stuck.} Red7 {Rather a waste of time but Black
is very cramped.} 19. Ne5 Re7 20. Rab1 {Increasing pressure on the QS.} Nd7 21.
Nxc6 bxc6 (21... Qxc6 22. Na5 $1) 22. Qa6 N7f6 (22... Nb8 23. Qa4) 23. Na5 Qc8
{Black must lose a Pawn.} 24. Qxc6 Rc7 25. Qa4 Qa6 26. Bf1 Qc8 27. Bg2 {
Black had to be prevented from playing 27.....Ne4} ({If...} 27. Bd3 {I
wouldn't be able to get the Bishop back on the h1-a8 diagonal.}) 27... Qa6 {
content to draw.} 28. Qb5 Qxb5 29. Rxb5 Ne7 30. Rc4 Rcd7 31. c6 Rc7 {The
d-Pawn is immune.} 32. Rcb4 (32. Rb7 {Is less decisive.}) 32... Nfd5 33. Bxd5
Nxd5 34. Rb8 Rdc8 35. Rxc8+ Rxc8 36. Rb7 a6 37. Ra7 Nc7 38. Nc4 {Black has too
many weaknesses to defend for long.} Nb5 39. Rxa6 Nxd4 40. Nd6 {Middlesex
finished 2nd on 8 behind Surrey on 9 [Hours of play 2.30 - 6pm] This game was
published in the S.C.C.U. Bulletin [March '62] "Nothing spectacular but just
steady pressure forcing the win of a pawn and then some nice play with a
Knight that forces Black's resignation. Passive play was Black's downfall". (S.
C.C.U.)} 1-0

