1. e4 c5 2. Nf3 d6 3. d4 cxd4 4. Nxd4 Nf6 5. Nc3 g6 6. f4 Nc6 7. Be3 Bg7 8. Qd2
a6 9. Be2 O-O 10. O-O Bd7 11. Bf3 Qc7 12. Kh1 {Preparing for a KS attack or
advance in the centre.} Rac8 {Black concentrates on White's weaknesses on c2
and the long diagonal.} 13. Nb3 {The Knight was well placed on d4 but Black
could play ...Na5--c4 to good advantage.} Na5 {Threatening 14.....Nc4 and also
giving himself more space, especially for his QBishop on the QS.} 14. Nxa5 Qxa5
15. Rab1 {Removing the Rook from the long diagonal and strengthening the
b-Pawn.} Bc6 16. Nd5 {Forcing Black to retreat and lose time.} Qxd2 $2 {
Giving up a pawn.} ({Correct was....} 16... Qd8 {but then he would be very
weak on b6 and White would have much the better game.}) 17. Nxe7+ Kh8 18. Bxd2
Rce8 19. Nxc6 bxc6 20. Rfe1 Rc8 21. Bc3 {Apart from the extra Pawn White has
the two Bishops and the better Pawn formation.} Rfe8 22. Rbd1 d5 23. exd5 Rxe1+
24. Rxe1 Nxd5 25. Bxg7+ Kxg7 26. Bxd5 cxd5 {White was obliged to make these
exchanges to keep the innitiative.} 27. Re2 {Defending the pawns and keeping
control of the second rank.} d4 {Threatening 28.....d6!!} 28. Kg1 a5 29. a4 Rb8
30. b3 d3 31. cxd3 Rxb3 32. Re5 Rxd3 33. Rxa5 h5 34. g3 h4 35. Kg2 hxg3 36.
hxg3 Kf6 {One would expect Black, who can place his Rook behind the passed
Pawn, to try to exchange off the KS Pawns.} 37. Kh3 Ke6 38. Re5+ Kd6 {This
leaves the KS Pawns weak.} 39. Kg4 f5+ {This creates even more weaknesses.} 40.
Kh4 Rd1 41. Kg5 {Black loses another Pawn. HCGS won 4 - 2.} 1-0

