1. e4 c5 2. Nf3 d6 3. h3 {Ultra passive.} Nc6 4. Nc3 g6 5. Bc4 Bg7 6. d3 a6 7.
Ng5 {Not a complete waste of time if he follows it up with f4.} e6 8. Qf3 {
Rather crude attacking play.} (8. f4 $142) 8... Nf6 9. a4 Nd4 10. Qd1 {White
is progressing backwards.} O-O 11. O-O b6 12. Bf4 Nh5 {Black's pieces are much
better placed than Whites} 13. Bd2 Ra7 {Prepares to bring the Rook onto a
centre file.} ({I didn't want to play....} 13... Bb7 {as it weakens the e-Pawn}
) 14. Rb1 {White prepares for a misguided QS advance. White does not have a
very good position but in any event he was stronger on the QS.} Nc6 {d4 was
not the best square for the Knight. This move prevents 14.b4 and allows a
possible .....Ne5.} 15. Na2 {White is determined to force b4 but this was a
mistaken plan.} b5 $1 {Putting an end to White's plan.} 16. axb5 axb5 17. Bxb5
$2 {White falls into a trap.} ({even after...} 17. Bb3 {his position was
hardly enviable.}) 17... Rxa2 18. Bxc6 Qb6 {Trapping the Bishop.} 19. e5 {
Cutting off my Knight's retreat square.} Qxc6 20. g4 {This move doesn't quite
regain the piece.} Bb7 21. Ne4 (21. f3 Ng3 22. Rf2 h6) 21... f5 22. gxh5 {
White's last hope is that he might open up my King's position sufficiently for
a possible perpetual check.} fxe4 23. hxg6 hxg6 24. Qg4 e3 {White can't last
any longer.} 25. fxe3 {White must either give up another piece or be mated. He
prefers not to prolong the agony.} (25. Qxe6+ Kh8 {is useless.}) 25... Qh1# 0-1

