1. e4 e6 2. d4 d5 3. Nd2 Nf6 4. e5 Nfd7 5. Bd3 c5 6. c3 Nc6 7. Ne2 {This
system for White doesn't promise much.} Qb6 8. Nf3 cxd4 9. cxd4 Bb4+ 10. Bd2
Bxd2+ 11. Qxd2 Qb4 {These exchanges relieve Black's position greatly and
secure at least an equal game. White now thought for a long time and played.}
12. Nc3 $2 {This gives up a couple of Pawns in order to gain an attack in the
centre and take advantage of Black's lack of QS development.} Nxd4 13. Nxd4
Qxd4 14. O-O Qxe5 15. Rfe1 Qh5 ({Not....} 15... Qc7 $2 16. Nxd5 $1) 16. Re3 {
Giving up a piece for one or two Pawns in order tomobilise his pieces quickly
and break open the position.} d4 17. Rh3 dxc3 18. Qxc3 Qe5 19. Qd2 g6 20. Re1
Qd6 {On d6 the Queen can't be attacked by the Rooks and prevents White playing
Qb4.} 21. Re4 b6 22. Qc3 e5 23. Bb5 O-O {At last the King gets to safety.} 24.
Rd3 Qc5 $1 ({Not so good is..} 24... Qe6) 25. Bxd7 (25. Qxc5 $2 Nxc5 $19) (25.
Rxd7 Bxd7 26. Qxc5 bxc5 27. Bxd7 Rfd8 $19) 25... Qxc3 26. bxc3 Rd8 27. Rxe5 (
27. Bb5 Rxd3 28. Bxd3 Bf5 29. Re3 Rd8 30. Bf1 Rd1 31. Z0 Bd3) 27... Bxd7 ({Not
} 27... Rxd7 $2 28. Re8+) 28. h4 (28. Red5 Re8) 28... Be6 29. Rxd8+ Rxd8 30. a3
Rd1+ 31. Kh2 Ra1 32. h5 Kg7 33. hxg6 hxg6 34. f4 Rxa3 35. Kg3 Rxc3+ 36. Kh4 a5
37. g4 a4 38. f5 gxf5 39. gxf5 Rc4+ 40. Kg5 f6+ 41. Kh5 Bf7# 0-1

