{Opponent info: GBR Co-Champ 1956; 4th= GBR Ch 1961} 1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 e6 3. Nc3
Bb4 4. Bg5 {I have never played against this move before but it didn't worry
me particularly.} c5 {I preffered this to the alternatives.} (4... d5) (4... h6
) 5. d5 exd5 6. cxd5 d6 {Now we are in a sort of Benoni position but with
Bishops on Kt5.} 7. e3 Bf5 {Solving the problem of the awkward QBishop.} 8. Bd3
Bxd3 (8... Bg6 9. Bb5+ Nbd7 10. Qa4 {is a nuisance.}) 9. Qxd3 Nbd7 10. Nge2 O-O
11. O-O Bxc3 {My KBishop is no longer any good especially if White were to
play 12.Ne4 followed by a3.} 12. bxc3 {White gets no play on the b-file after
this.} ({I expected} 12. Qxc3) 12... Qc7 {Unpinning the Knight and threatening
13.....c4} 13. c4 Ne5 14. Qc2 ({If} 14. Qf5 {I intended....} Nxc4 $6 15. Bxf6
gxf6 16. Qxf6 Ne5 {With interesting play.}) 14... Nfd7 {Leaving the Bishop
stranded and threatening to win his c-Pawn.} 15. f4 {Leaves the e-pawn weak.}
Ng4 (15... Ng6 {Passive.}) 16. Rf3 Rfe8 {Taking advantsge of the weak e-Pawn &
a Rook into play.} (16... Rae8 {I didn't play this as I might need f8 for the
Knight}) 17. Qc3 f6 {Easing the pressure.} 18. Bh4 Nh6 ({Not...} 18... Nf8 $2
19. Rg3 Nh6 20. Bxf6 $1) 19. Ng3 Qd8 {Bringing the Queen back into play and
overprotecting the f-Pawn.} 20. Nh5 Nf5 {My position suddenly improves now.}
21. Bf2 Qe7 22. Rh3 {He gets no advantage from the h-file.} g6 23. g4 Ng7 24.
Nxg7 Qxg7 25. Rb1 b6 26. Qd3 Qe7 27. Kf1 Qe4 28. Qxe4 ({Not} 28. Ke2 $2 Qxf4)
28... Rxe4 29. Rc1 g5 30. Rf3 ({Not} 30. fxg5 $2 fxg5 31. Z0 Ne5) 30... Rae8
31. Rc3 gxf4 32. exf4 Nf8 (32... Re2 {Not as good.}) 33. Rce3 Ng6 (33... Rxe3 {
Thetext is better.}) 34. f5 Rxe3 35. Bxe3 (35. Rxe3 Rxe3 36. Bxe3 Ne5) 35...
Ne5 36. Rg3 Nxc4 37. Bh6 Re5 38. Kg2 Re2+ 39. Kh3 Rxa2 {This was a
simultaneous display against all London Championship prizewinners (33 Bds)} 0-1

