{Opponent info: International Master; GBR Champion 1956.} 1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 e6 3.
Nc3 c5 4. d5 exd5 5. cxd5 d6 6. Bg5 {Not the most usual.} Be7 7. e3 $2 {
This gives up a Pawn.} Nxd5 8. Bxe7 ({If} 8. Qxd5 Bxg5 9. Ne4 Be7 10. Bc4 O-O
11. Rd1 Qb6) 8... Nxe7 (8... Nxc3 {Was playable.}) 9. Bb5+ Nbc6 10. Nf3 a6 ({
I considered...} 10... Be6 {but White can play...} 11. Ng5) 11. Ba4 {This
allows me to advance on the QS and gain some space.} b5 12. Bc2 d5 $1 {
Eradicating the weakness on d6 and e5 and gaining still more space.} 13. O-O
Bg4 {Rather more enterprising then the alternatives.} (13... O-O) ({If} 13...
Be6 {White can play} 14. Ng5 {quite well.}) 14. h3 {This weakens the KS but I
was threatening 15.....Ne5} Bh5 {Maintaining the pin. Better than taking the
Knight} (14... Bxf3) 15. g4 {Weakens the KS terribly but I was threatening 15..
...Ne5} Bg6 16. Kg2 {A necessary defensive measure.} Qd6 {Aiming at the KS.}
17. Qd2 O-O-O {This is perfectly safe and has the advantage of overprotecting
the d-Pawn and prepares a KS attack.} 18. Rac1 f5 {Opening lines to the White
King.} 19. g5 {Trying to keep the position blocked.} Bh5 {Piling on the
pressure. I am threatening ...Ne5, Qg6, h6} 20. Ne2 h6 21. Nf4 $2 {This loses
outright but his position was already beyond saving.} Bxf3+ 22. Kxf3 hxg5 23.
Nd3 Rxh3+ 24. Ke2 (24. Kg2 $4 Qh2#) 24... c4 25. Ne1 d4 26. Ng2 {Loses a piece.
.} ({but..} 26. exd4 Nxd4+ 27. Kd1 Qe5 {Is worse.}) 26... d3+ 27. Bxd3 Qxd3+
28. Qxd3 Rxd3 29. Rh1 Rh6 30. b3 Rdd6 31. Rxh6 gxh6 32. bxc4 bxc4 33. Rxc4 h5
34. Ne1 h4 {Simultaneous display against players from school's chess league 
(24Bds) He won 18 drew 3 and lost 3 in this display. For winning I won a
programme signed by: Gligoric, Szabo, Olaffson, O'Kelly, Toran, Penrose,
Alexander, Clarke, Horseman, Wade, Larsen. Wade "He attacked me from the word
go and just left me absolutely no chances for counter-play"} 0-1

