1. c4 f5 2. d4 e6 3. Nc3 Nf6 4. Nf3 Bb4 5. Bd2 O-O 6. g3 Qe8 7. Bg2 c6 8. O-O
d5 (8... d6 $142 {intending ....e5 and preventing 9.Nb5}) 9. Nxd5 Nxd5 10. cxd5
Bxd2 11. Qxd2 exd5 12. Ne5 Qe7 13. Rac1 Nd7 14. Nxd7 {This is probably wrong.}
(14. f4 $142) 14... Bxd7 15. e3 {Strengthening the centre against 15.....f4 .
However immediate counteraction on the QS was necessary.} Rf6 {Preparing to
double Rooks or play 16.....Rg6 or Rh6} 16. a3 {Allowing the Queen to move
without allowing Black to play .....Qb5. However energetic QS play was called
for.} Qf7 17. Qe2 {Keeping an eye on h5 but Black is progressing faster.} Rf8
18. Rc3 {Strengthening the 3rd rank after any Pawn exchanges which may occur
but a QS Pawn advance was better.} g5 19. f4 {Black threatened 19.....f4
opening the KS with disatrous consequences.} g4 20. Kf2 {The best place for
the King it was now too late for a QS advance.} Re8 21. Qd2 Qe7 22. Re1 {
Overprotecting the e-Pawn.} h5 23. Rcc1 {White can do nothing but defend
passively} Re6 24. Qd3 Kg7 25. Qd2 h4 26. Qd3 Rh6 27. Rh1 {Not really
necessary.} Reh8 28. Qc3 {It would have been better to keep an eye on e5.} b6
29. b4 Re6 30. Rce1 (30. Rhe1 $142) 30... Kg6 {At this point I forgot his plan
and wasted two moves.} 31. Re2 Kg7 32. Ree1 Re8 33. Re2 Rh6 34. Ree1 $2 {
Loses outright.} h3 $1 35. Bf1 Qe4 36. Kg1 Rhe6 37. Qd3 Qxd3 38. Bxd3 Rxe3 {
5 This shows that passive defence won't hold in a cramped position. Hayes lost
the match 3 -} 0-1

