{Opponent info: Grade 5b (ECF 169-176)} 1. e4 c5 2. Nf3 d6 3. c3 Nf6 4. e5 dxe5
5. Nxe5 Qd6 6. d4 Nbd7 {Threatening to win a Pawn.} 7. Bb5 cxd4 8. cxd4 Qd5 9.
Bc4 (9. Bxd7+ {is more streight forward but les enterprising.}) 9... Qxg2 10.
Bxf7+ (10. Nxf7 {Loses.}) 10... Kd8 11. Qf3 Qxf3 12. Nxf3 Nb6 {Now ....e6 is
in the air trapping White's Bishop.} 13. Nc3 e6 14. Bg5 {This gives the Bishop
a retreat square on h5.} Bb4 15. Bh5 Nbd5 16. Rc1 Bd7 17. Ne5 Ke7 18. O-O Bxc3
19. bxc3 Be8 {If I let White play 20.NxB he would have two Bishops against two
Knights .19.....Be8 also strengthens f6 & g6.} 20. Bf3 h6 21. Bd2 {White
doesn't want to give up the two Bishops} b5 {I must play this or 22.c4 puts me
up the creek.} 22. c4 bxc4 23. Nxc4 (23. Rxc4 Bb5 24. Nc6+ {is playable.})
23... Rb8 {Unpinning my Knight.} 24. Rfe1 Bh5 {Rather risky but it clears the
air.} 25. Bxh5 Nxh5 26. Ne5 {Threatening two forks.} Ke8 27. Nc6 Rb2 28. Rxe6+
Kd7 {White suddenly finds that he isn't as well off as he thought.} 29. Re5
Nhf4 {White is suddenly in a mess. His Bishop is attacked and he is threatened
with possible forks on e2 or d3 so:} 30. Rxd5+ Nxd5 31. Ne5+ {[62']} Ke6 {[63']
} 32. Rc6+ Kf5 33. Nc4 Rxa2 34. f3 {Reducing my Kings squares.} Ra1+ 35. Kf2
Nf6 36. Rc7 Ra4 37. Rc5+ Ke6 38. Bc3 Rf8 {Threatening 39.....Ne4+} 39. d5+ Kf7
40. Ne5+ Kg8 41. Ng6 $2 {[81'] Overlooks Black's reply but he was in a lost
position anyway.} Ne4+ {[86']} 42. Ke3 Nxc5 {Apparently White wants to play to
the bitter end.} 43. d6 Re8+ 44. Ne7+ Kf7 45. Be5 Nd7 46. Kd3 Rxe7 47. dxe7
Kxe7 48. Bxg7 Rh4 49. Bd4 a6 50. Bg1 Ne5+ 51. Ke3 a5 52. Bf2 Rxh2 53. Bg3 Rh5
54. f4 Rh3 55. Kf2 a4 56. fxe5 Rxg3 {Quite interesting up until move 41 but
White was lost before then. Middlesex won 13.5 - 6.5} 0-1

