1. e4 e6 2. d4 d5 3. Nc3 Bb4 4. e5 c5 5. a3 Bxc3+ 6. bxc3 Qc7 7. Qg4 Ne7 8.
Qxg7 Rg8 9. Qxh7 cxd4 10. Rb1 (10. Ne2 {is more usual.}) 10... Qxe5+ {This
shows my unfamiliarity with the variation.} (10... dxc3 {is better as White's
e-Pawn remains weak.}) 11. Ne2 dxc3 12. Bf4 {Forcing off Queens and getting a
good endgame. White's h-Pawn is tremendously strong after the Queen exchange.}
Qf5 13. Qxf5 Nxf5 14. Nxc3 a6 {White threatened 15.Nb5, 16.Nb7} 15. Bd3 {
Now White's Bishops aid the advance of the h-Pawn.} Ne7 (15... Nh4 {is no good
after} 16. Bg3) 16. h4 {Simply threatening to advance up the file to h7,
combined with Bh6-Bg7} b5 (16... Rxg2 {Seems too risky.}) (16... Nd7 {was also
good.}) 17. h5 Nd7 {I was really worried at this stage.} 18. Kd2 ({If} 18. h6
f5 {but what the outcome would be I don't know although I can't see anything
definite for White.}) 18... e5 {Black's position suddenly looks better.} 19.
Rbe1 $6 {Whether this is good enough when correctly followed up I don't know.
Certainly it is unsound as followed up n the game.} d4 ({Not} 19... exf4 $2 20.
Nxd5 $1) 20. Ne4 {This seems best.} exf4 21. Nd6+ Kf8 22. Re4 $2 {This is
wrong.} ({Correct is} 22. Rh4 {e.g.} Nc5 23. Rxf4 {Black can't play..} Be6 $2 {
...due to} 24. Rxe6) 22... Nc5 23. Rxf4 Be6 {Black's position is now fairly
strong. White's on;y hope is the h-Pawn.} 24. Re1 {This really shows how 22.
Rh4 was best as now White is a move behind.} Nxd3 25. cxd3 Nd5 {The threat was
26.RxB} 26. Rxd4 Rxg2 27. Ne4 Rh2 28. Rb1 Rxh5 29. Nc3 Nxc3 30. Kxc3 Ke7 31. a4
Rc8+ 32. Kd2 Rcc5 33. Rg1 Rhf5 34. f4 a5 35. axb5 Rxb5 36. Rg8 Rb4 37. Rxb4
axb4 38. Ke3 Rb5 39. Rg2 b3 40. Rb2 {[90']} Rb4 {34 [55'] Middlesex won 46-34}
0-1

