{Opponent info: S.C.C.U. U-16 Co-Champion 1962} 1. e4 Nf6 2. Nc3 {White is
unwilling to take the aggressive course.} d6 (2... e5 {would transpose to the
Vienna game.}) 3. d4 g6 4. Nf3 (4. e5 {Loses a Pawn.}) 4... Bg7 5. Bc4 O-O 6.
O-O a6 7. a4 c5 8. Be3 cxd4 9. Bxd4 (9. Nxd4 {Transposing into a normal
Sicillian variation would be better.}) 9... Bg4 {Taking the innitiative.} 10.
h3 Bxf3 11. Qxf3 Nc6 12. Ne2 $2 {This turna out badly as the long Black
diagonal is weakened.} Nxd4 13. Nxd4 Nd7 $1 {Now White has to be careful.} 14.
Qe3 {Now he loses a Pawn.} ({Better was} 14. c3 Qb6 15. Nb3 Rac8 16. a5) 14...
Qb6 15. Rfd1 {[36']} Qxb2 {[18'] This is not idle Pawn grabbing but the result
of White's artificial play.} 16. Rab1 Bxd4 17. Qxd4 {Better than taking the
Queen.} (17. Rxb2) 17... Qxd4 18. Rxd4 {White threatens a5, Bd5, R4b4 so the
Pawn can't stay on b7.} b6 {The best move I think.} (18... Nc5 19. a5 {is no
good for Black.}) 19. f4 {Intending an eventual e5.} Ra7 {White must not be
allowed to play Bd5 with game of tempo followed by Bc6 & BxN as my Knight is
essential to the defence of the b-Pawn.} 20. Bd5 Rb8 21. Rdb4 {Now 22.Bc6
really is threatened.} (21. Bc6 Nc5 22. Z0 Rc7 {is a waste of time.}) 21... Rc7
22. R1b2 ({Not} 22. c4 $4 e6) 22... Rc5 23. Kf2 {[56'] Now he gets really tied
up but his position wasn't very good anyway.} Rbc8 {[39']} 24. Bb3 {Forced.}
Rc3 {Keeping White tied up.} 25. e5 {Loses a Pawn but White is desperate.} dxe5
26. fxe5 e6 {Prevents 27.e6} 27. Rd4 {He couldn't save the Pawn.} Nxe5 28. Rd6
{[67']} Nc4 {Winning straight off.} 29. Bxc4 R8xc4 30. Rbxb6 {White sees the
chance of getting both Rooks on the 7th with a chance of perpetual check.} Rxa4
31. Rd7 Rf4+ {defending the f-Pawn.} 32. Kg1 Rxc2 33. Rxa6 Rff2 34. g4 Kg7 35.
Raa7 {White has apparently decided to keep trying.} Rf4 36. Re7 {Threatening
37.Rxe6} Re2 37. Rab7 g5 {Preventing 38.g5 which would have nuisance value.}
38. Rbd7 Kg6 39. Re8 h6 40. Rdd8 {White succeeds in creating slight
counterchances but to no avail.} Ra4 41. Kf1 Rb2 42. Rd1 Rab4 {Threatening to
exchange off one of the Rooks.} 43. Rdd8 Ra4 {Black decides on a different
method.} 44. Rd1 Ra3 45. Kg1 Rg3+ {So as to take the h-Pawn with check or with
the threat of mate.} 46. Kf1 Rxh3 47. Kg1 Rg3+ 48. Kh1 Rxg4 49. Rf1 {White
sees that he will be stalemated if he sacrifices both Rooks. Therefore.....}
Rf4 50. Re1 g4 {White plays on as he has slight drawing chances if he can gat
in a stalemate position and then sacrifice both his Rooks.} 51. Rg1 Kh5 52. Rg8
Kh4 53. Rh8 h5 54. Rg8 Kh3 55. Rg5 Rh2# {Quite a good game.} 0-1

