{Opponent info: Grade 3b (201-208 ECF)} 1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bb5 a6 4. Ba4
Nf6 5. O-O Be7 6. Re1 b5 7. Bb3 O-O 8. c3 d6 9. h3 Na5 10. Bc2 c5 11. d4 Qc7
12. Nbd2 Bd7 13. Nf1 cxd4 14. cxd4 Nc6 15. Be3 {This seemed better than 15.d5
which blocks the position and may allow Black to counterattack with .....f5
subsequently.} (15. d5) 15... Rfe8 ({If now....} 15... exd4 {then I am much
better off as the centre exchange would be favourable to me.}) 16. Ng3 Rac8 {
Threatening 17.....Nb4} 17. Rc1 Qb7 {Hoping to gain some pressure on the long
diagonal and also remove the Queen from the same file as my Rook.} 18. d5 {
Blocking the long diagonal and lessening the scope of his pieces somewhat.} Nb4
19. Bb1 {Defending the a-pawn, e-Pawn and the d3 square.} (19. Bb3 Rxc1 20.
Qxc1 $2 Nd3) 19... a5 {making a retreat for the Knight.} 20. Qd2 {'Observing'
the a-Pawn and allowing me to answer ......RxR with RxR.} Ra8 {Envisaging the
opening of the QS files.} 21. a3 Na6 22. Qxa5 {To 'play safe' by doubling
Rooks would be rather difficult and possibly a waste of time.} b4 (22... Nc5
23. Qc3) 23. Qb6 Qxb6 24. Bxb6 Reb8 25. Ba5 {Perfectly safe and threatening to
hang onto the Pawn. 25.Be3 didn't look particularly good.} (25. Be3) 25... Rb5
{Regains the Pawn.} ({If...} 25... bxa3 {I may be able to keep the Pawn.}) 26.
axb4 Nxb4 27. Bxb4 Rxb4 {Handing back the Pawn straight away as I can't defend
it anyway.} 28. Bd3 Rb3 {Better than the variation below.} (28... Rxb2 29. Rb1
Raa2 30. Rxb2 Rxb2 31. Rb1) 29. Red1 Rab8 30. Rb1 Bd8 {Draw agreed on
adjournment.} 1/2-1/2

[Event "Hayes & Harlington Challenge Cup"]
