1. e4 c5 2. Nf3 d6 3. d4 cxd4 4. Nxd4 Nf6 5. Nc3 g6 6. f4 Nc6 7. Nxc6 bxc6 8.
e5 {I played this variation as I knew my opponent was unfamiliar with it.} dxe5
(8... Nd7 {is more usual here.}) 9. Qxd8+ Kxd8 10. fxe5 Ng4 {Black's attempt
to win my e-Pawn doesn't turn out too well.} ({If} 10... Nd7 11. e6 {Seems
quite good for White.}) (10... Nd5 {seems a reasonable alternative.}) 11. Bf4
Bg7 12. O-O-O+ Ke8 {The obvious move in view of the fact that on c7 the King
is very open, especially to such things as an eventual e6+. However after my
reply Black discovers the drawbacks of 12.....Ke8} (12... Kc7) (12... Bd7 {
M.C.O (9th Edn)}) 13. Nb5 $3 (13. Be2 Nxe5 14. Rhe1 $1 (14. Rd4 $1)) (13. Re1
g5 $1) (13. e6 fxe6 14. Na4 e5 15. Bd2 (15. Be2 h5 16. Bd2) 15... Bf5) 13...
Bxe5 {This loses but there seems no alternative.} (13... cxb5 14. Bxb5+ Bd7 15.
Bxd7+ Kf8 16. Bxg4 $18) (13... Bd7 14. Nc7+ Kd8 15. e6 $1 fxe6 16. Bg3 {
and Black's best is.....} Rc8 17. Nxe6+ Ke8 18. Nxg7+ $18) (13... f6 14. Nc7+
Kf7 15. Bc4+ e6 16. exf6 Bxf6 17. Nxa8 Nf2 18. Nc7 $18) (13... e6 14. Nc7+ Ke7
15. Bg5+ f6 16. exf6+ Bxf6 17. Bxf6+ Kxf6 18. Be2 $1 $18) 14. Bxe5 Nxe5 15.
Nc7+ Kf8 16. Rd8+ {Obviously it is easy for White with two more Rooks off.} Kg7
17. Rxh8 Kxh8 18. Nxa8 1-0

