1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bb5 a6 4. Ba4 d6 5. c3 Bd7 6. O-O g6 7. Re1 Bg7 {
I had never played this defence before but I only needed a draw from this game
so it seemed a reasonable defence to play.} 8. d4 exd4 ({I considered....} 8...
f6 {here as in some variations of this defence , but 8.....PxP seemed equally
as good.}) 9. Nxd4 {This does not seem particularly better than 9.PxP} (9. cxd4
) 9... Nxd4 10. Bxd7+ Qxd7 11. cxd4 Ne7 12. Nc3 Nc6 13. d5 {This move gives
space to White but gives Black the long diagonal.} Ne7 (13... Ne5 14. f4 Ng4 (
14... Nc4 {is also playable.})) 14. Be3 O-O 15. Bd4 Be5 16. Bxe5 dxe5 17. Rc1
Rfd8 {I intend the freeing move .....c6 but the Queen needs prior protection.}
18. Qb3 c6 19. Red1 ({Rather stronger seems....} 19. dxc6 Nxc6 20. Nd5 {
Threatening forks on b6 & f6}) 19... cxd5 20. Nxd5 Nxd5 21. Rxd5 Qe7 22. Rdc5 {
The d-file with the outpost d5 seems the best file for the Rooks.} Rd7 23. g3
Rad8 24. Qc3 Rd4 25. Rc7 R8d7 {1 Here I offered a draw which he accepted.
Scores in the Final Group Were R.A.C [Sect A winner] ----- 2.5 P.J.Adams [Sect
B Winner]1.5 P.J.Holt [Runner up A] ----1 D.H.Smith [Runner up B] -} ({I
considered....} 25... Qb4 {...but White has a good reply in...} 26. Qf3)
1/2-1/2

