1. e4 e6 2. d4 d5 3. Nc3 Nf6 4. Bg5 dxe4 5. Nxe4 Be7 6. Bxf6 Bxf6 7. Nf3 O-O 8.
c3 Nd7 9. Bd3 {Black now gets in the thematic freeing move .....e5} e5 10. d5 {
More double edged than......} ({......} 10. O-O) 10... a6 11. c4 {Supports the
d-Pawn but allows Black the next manoeuvre.} Be7 {Threatening 12......f5 12.N
moves e4. The opening now takes on a somewhat usual character.} 12. g4 $1 Bb4+
13. Kf1 Re8 {ntending to force his f-Pawn forward and then advance his e-Pawn.}
14. g5 f5 15. gxf6 Nxf6 (15... gxf6 16. Ng3 Z0 17. Be4 Z0 18. Nf5) 16. h3 {
Prevents Bishop or Knight landing on g4.} Bf5 ({Good seems} 16... Nh5 17. Z0
Nf4) 17. Qc2 Nxe4 18. Bxe4 Bxe4 19. Qxe4 Qf6 20. Rd1 Rf8 21. Rd3 Rae8 22. Rg1
Qf4 {Black's exchange policy merely develops White's pieces.} 23. Rg4 Qc1+ 24.
Kg2 Rf4 25. Rxf4 Qxf4 26. Re3 Qxe4 {This turns out badly as his e-Pawn is weak
but he nolonger had any choice.} 27. Rxe4 Bd6 28. Nd4 g6 29. f4 Re7 30. Ne6 {
Threatening to win outright with 31.c5} exf4 31. c5 f3+ $8 32. Kxf3 Rf7+ 33.
Kg2 $1 ({Not} 33. Ke2 $2 Bg3 34. Z0 Rf2+ $140) 33... Bf8 34. Nxf8 Kxf8 35. Rb4
b5 36. cxb6 cxb6 37. Rxb6 Rd7 38. Rxa6 Rxd5 39. Rf6+ $1 Kg7 ({I don't know why
he didn't play....} 39... Ke7 {..not that it makes any difference}) 40. Rf2 Ra5
41. b4 Ra6 42. b5 Rb6 43. a4 (43. a4 {White wins easily e.g.} h6 44. Rb2 Kf6
45. a5 Rb8 46. b6 Ke6 47. b7 Kd6 48. a6 Kc7 49. a7 Rxb7 50. Rc2+ $1) 1-0

