1. e4 c5 2. Nf3 d6 3. d4 cxd4 4. Nxd4 Nf6 5. Nc3 a6 6. Bc4 e6 7. Bb3 Be7 8. f4
Nc6 9. Be3 Qc7 10. Qf3 b5 11. a3 ({Unfortunately} 11. e5 {is unplayable.})
11... Nxd4 12. Bxd4 Bb7 13. O-O-O O-O 14. g4 {Black is now in a precarious
positionon the KS so he tries a Pawn sacrifice.} b4 $5 15. axb4 d5 16. e5 Ne4
17. Nxe4 dxe4 18. Qc3 (18. Qe3 {followed by...} Z0 19. Bb6 {is probably better.
}) 18... Qxc3 19. Bxc3 (19. bxc3 a5) 19... e3 20. Rhg1 ({I also considered} 20.
Rhe1 {but this leaves the g-Pawn weak.}) 20... Rfd8 21. Rxd8+ Rxd8 22. Rd1 $4 {
A stupid move, the result of not doing proper analysis. I wouldn't have made
this mistake in an actual game.} Rxd1+ 23. Kxd1 Bf3+ 24. Kc1 (24. Ke1 Bh4+)
24... Bh4 25. Bc4 e2 26. Bxa6 e1=Q+ 27. Bxe1 Bxe1 28. b5 Ba5 29. c4 Be2 30. b3
f6 {Black creates a passed Pawn.} 31. exf6 gxf6 32. Kb2 e5 33. fxe5 fxe5 34.
Bb7 Bd3 35. Kc1 {White has to bring the King back to stop the Pawn} e4 36. Kd1
Kg7 37. c5 e3 38. Bf3 Bxb5 39. Be2 Bc6 40. Kc2 Bd2 41. Kb2 {Hoping to get in
Ka3 & b4.} Bb4 42. h4 Bxc5 43. Kc3 {The b-Pawn gets going but not very far.}
Kf6 44. b4 Bb6 45. b5 Bb7 46. Kd3 Ke5 47. h5 Kf4 {This game also suffered
fromlack of proper analysis, necessary for correspondence play. Results of the
A.P.A. A8 1962 1st D.R.Bates 8/10 2nd W.A.Watson 7.5} 0-1

