1. e4 c5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. d4 cxd4 4. Nxd4 Nf6 5. Nc3 d6 6. Bc4 e6 7. Bb3 Be7 8.
Be3 O-O 9. f4 Bd7 10. Ndb5 {Rather too ambitous. The Black Queenproves to be
quite well placed on b8.} Qb8 11. Qf3 {The Queen would be better on d2, or d3
then the e-Pawn would not be pinned.} a6 12. Nd4 b5 13. a3 Nxd4 14. Bxd4 Bc6 {
Now my e-Pawn comes under fire.} 15. O-O-O Qb7 16. Bxf6 {The alternative is
todefend the e-Pawn with a Rook.} Bxf6 17. Rxd6 a5 18. Ne2 {The Knight would
have to move anyway. Now I have the option of playing a4 after .....b4} a4 19.
Ba2 Bxe4 20. Qf2 Qc7 21. Rd2 b4 22. axb4 {I am more or less obliged to play
this.} a3 23. b3 {Althought this buries my Bishop it seems the only way to
keep the position closed.} Bc3 {Black based his attack on the assumption that
it won the exchange in view of 24.NxB QxN threatening .....Qb2+ and QxB} ({
Possiblt better is....} 23... Bb2+ 24. Kb1 {when my c-Pawn is weak.}) 24. Nxc3
Qxc3 25. Qd4 {The move Black had overlooked.} Rac8 ({Not} 25... Qxd4 26. Rxd4 {
followed by} Z0 27. c4 {probably wins.}) 26. Re1 $5 {Whether this is better
than 26.QxQ is hard to say.} (26. Qxc3 Rxc3 27. Re1 f5 {followed by...} 28. Z0
Rfc8 {and I am tied to the defence of the c-Pawn.}) 26... Qxd4 (26... Qxc2+ {
Doesn't work} 27. Rxc2 Rxc2+ 28. Kb1 $1 {etc.}) 27. Rxd4 Rxc2+ 28. Kb1 Bg6 29.
g4 $4 {A horrible blunder due to lack of concentration. I still can't seem to
keep my concentration to a great enough extent. I realised my blunder straight
away.} Rd2+ {A ridiculous blunderat the end which spoilttha game although I
played without enough thought throughought.} 0-1

