1. c4 Nf6 2. g3 g6 3. Bg2 Bg7 4. d4 O-O 5. Nc3 d6 6. e4 Nbd7 7. Nge2 e5 8. O-O
exd4 9. Nxd4 c6 10. h3 {To prevent ......Ng5 after Be3} a5 11. Be3 Nc5 12. Qc2
Re8 13. Nb3 Nxb3 {After this Black suffers from weaknesses on the QS Black
squares.} ({Possibly better was....} 13... Na6) (13... Be6 14. Nxc5 dxc5 15.
Bxc5 Bxc4 16. Rfd1 Qb8 17. Na4 Be6 18. Nb6 Ra6 19. f4 {(Botvinnik - Salo 1954)}
) 14. axb3 {Rather better than 14.QxN} (14. Qxb3) 14... Bd7 {Black's position
was rather uncomftable, now his d-Pawn comes under pressure.} 15. Rfd1 Bf8 16.
Bf4 Qc8 17. Kh2 Re6 18. Na4 {Taking advantage of his Black square weakness.}
Ra6 19. Rd4 {so as to double Rooks and strengthen the e-Pawn.} Nh5 {This
doesn't achieve much.} 20. Be3 Re8 {Black can donothing.} 21. Rad1 {Threatens
22.g4 followed by 24.Bf4 winning the d-Pawn} b5 {Energetic but to no avail.}
22. Nc3 bxc4 23. bxc4 Be6 24. Na4 f5 {Again energetic but it doesn't relieve
the pressure.} 25. c5 $1 dxc5 26. Nxc5 Bxc5 27. Qxc5 fxe4 28. Bxe4 Nf6 (28...
Bxh3 {was alltogether too rash especially as he was in time trouble....} 29.
Bd3 Ra8 30. Bc4+ {etc.}) 29. Bd3 Ra8 30. Qg5 $1 Kg7 31. Rf4 $1 Qd8 32. Bxg6 $6
({I considered and Black suggested....} 32. Bd4 Rf8 33. Bxg6 {The point being
that Black can't play 33.....Ng4+. This appears to win for White even against..
.} h6 34. Bxf6+ Rxf6 35. Rxf6) 32... Ng4+ ({If...} 32... h6 33. Qe5) 33. Rxg4
Qxg5 34. Rxg5 hxg6 35. Ra1 (35. Rd6 {Alaso looked good.}) 35... Bd5 36. Rg4 {
Threatening 37.R4a4 [Adjourned] Play resumed 30/01/63} Bb3 {[Sealed move.] and
best.} (36... Re4 37. Rxe4 Bxe4 38. Ra4 {Wins a Pawn.}) (36... Be4 37. Bd2 $1
Bf5 (37... a4 38. Re1 Bc2 39. Rxe8 Rxe8 40. Rc4) 38. Rc4 Re2 39. Bxa5) 37. Re1
{The best move.} Rad8 (37... Red8 {If the Rook moves off the e-file for
example....} 38. Bd4+ Kf7 39. Rf4+ Kg8 40. Re7 {Wins easily for White.}) 38.
Bd4+ Kf7 39. Rf4+ Kg8 40. Rxe8+ Rxe8 41. Rf6 Re6 42. Rxe6 Bxe6 {All this was
forced but now Black has a lost endgame.} 43. g4 Kf7 44. Kg3 Bd5 45. Kf4 a4 46.
h4 Bb3 {Black can achieve nothing better than moving his Bishop back and forth.
} 47. Kg5 Bc2 48. f4 Bb3 49. h5 gxh5 50. Kxh5 Be6 51. f5 Bd7 {so as to hinder
g5 but it makes no difference in the long run.} 52. Kg5 c5 {This gives his
Bishop more scope but makes no difefrence to the result.} 53. Bxc5 Bb5 54. Kf4
Bc4 55. g5 Bd3 56. g6+ Kg8 57. Kg5 Bc4 58. Kf6 Bd3 59. Bd4 Bc2 60. Kg5 Bb3 61.
f6 Bc4 62. Kf4 $1 Bb3 63. Ke5 {He will have to sacrifice his Bishop for the
two Pawns and my King gets to the QS first. He would have lost just the same
even had his Pawn still been on c6. Uni lost the match 2 - 3 A good game.} 1-0

