1. e4 e6 2. f4 {This unusual move is the 3rd move in the 2.Qe2 variation.
Apparentl White was trying it out on move 2.} d5 3. e5 c5 {Black has probably
equalised already.} 4. d4 cxd4 5. Qxd4 Nc6 6. Bb5 Bd7 7. Bxc6 Bxc6 8. Nf3 Ne7
9. c3 {Strengthening d4 but denying the square to his Knight.} Nf5 10. Qf2 Qa5
{Intending 11.....Bc5} 11. b4 {Although this prevents the Bishop move to c5 it
leaves a hole on c4, leaves his c-Pawn weak and provides a target when I play
a5.} Qc7 (11... Bxb4 {seems rash as I secure an advantage anyway.}) 12. Bb2 {
Strengthens the c-Pawn, but he would have done better to castle while he had
the chance.} a5 13. a3 {Relatively best.} Bb5 {Prevents KS castling.} 14. Nd4
Nxd4 15. Qxd4 a4 16. Nd2 Be7 {Intending .....0-0 .....f6 opening the f-file.}
17. h4 {White has nothing better than to push his Pawns.} O-O 18. g4 f6 19.
O-O-O {Again as good as anything.} Rac8 {Strengthening the position before
breaking it open.} 20. Nf3 $2 {An oversight but his position was difficult
anyway. Black threatens to penetrate on the f-file.} Be2 21. Rh3 Bxd1 22. Kxd1
Qc4 23. Qxc4 Rxc4 24. Nd4 fxe5 25. fxe5 (25. Nxe6 {I intended...} Rfxf4) 25...
Rf1+ 26. Kc2 Kf7 27. Bc1 Rc8 28. g5 Bd8 29. Bd2 Bc7 30. Nf3 Ke7 31. Be3 b6 32.
Bd4 Rf8 33. Nd2 R1f5 34. Kd3 Kd7 35. b5 {Otherwise I play.....b5 and probably
win the e-Pawn. However I win a Pawn this way as well.} Bxe5 36. Bxb6 Rb8 37.
Bc5 Rxb5 38. Bb4 Bd6 39. Nf3 e5 40. Kc2 Bxb4 41. axb4 Kd6 42. Kb2 Rb8 43. Ka3
Ra8 44. Ne1 e4 45. Nc2 Rf3 {1 I kept my concentration much better than normal
in this game but still tended to play somewhat quickly. Uni won the match 4 -}
0-1

