1. e4 e6 2. d4 d5 3. Nc3 Bb4 4. a3 Bxc3+ 5. bxc3 Ne7 6. Qg4 Ng6 7. exd5 {
This move seems doubtful.} (7. e5 $142) 7... Qxd5 8. c4 Qd6 9. c5 {This move
too seems suspect} Qd8 10. Bc4 e5 11. Qf3 O-O ({Also good is...} 11... Qe7 {
which I considered.}) 12. d5 {White's position begins to look a bit shakey.} c6
13. Bd2 (13. d6 {fails against.} Qa5+) 13... Nh4 $1 {Giving White more
headaches. The Knight is to be manoeuvered to d5.} 14. Qb3 ({If} 14. Qe4 Bf5)
14... cxd5 (14... Nxg2+ 15. Kf1 Nh4 {allows..} 16. d6) 15. Bxd5 Nc6 {Taking
control of d5.} 16. Ne2 Nf5 17. O-O-O {White, realising his QS Pawns are weak,
tries for a KS attack} Qe7 {Black now begins a Qs attack whilst putting
pressure on White's weak Pawns. Also the e-Pawn is strengthened.} 18. Qc4 Bd7
19. Ng3 Nfd4 20. Rhe1 b6 21. Ne4 {Threatening 22.Nd6} ({If} 21. cxb6 Qxa3+ {
is very strong.}) 21... Be6 {rEmoving White's good Bishop so as to play .....
f6 later on.} 22. Bxe6 Nxe6 23. Re3 bxc5 24. Rg3 Kh8 25. Ng5 Nxg5 26. Bxg5 f6
27. Be3 Nd4 28. Rh3 ({If} 28. c3 Nf5 $1 29. Bxc5 Qc7 {and wins}) 28... Rab8 29.
Rd3 Qb7 30. Bxd4 cxd4 31. Qe6 {White misses a forced draw.} ({I had calculated
that if....} 31. Rxh7+ Kxh7 32. Rh3+ Kg6 33. Qe6 $3 {White had a dead draw,
and Black would have to play well in order not to lose.} (33. Rg3+ Kf5 34. Rf3+
Qxf3 {with winning chances})) 31... g6 32. Rb3 Qg7 {The other variations below
only draw at best. White now gets more play.} (32... Qe4) (32... Qxg2) 33. Qd6
(33. Rxh7+ {is too risky.}) 33... Rbc8 34. Rbg3 g5 35. Qe6 {He offered a draw
here.} Qc7 36. Qf5 Rf7 37. Rh6 Rcf8 38. Rgh3 Qe7 39. Kd2 Qc7 40. g4 $2 {
White thought that he had me completely tied up here and that the game was a
dead draw. Hence the indifferent move which weakened him in the endgame. He
offered me another draw here. Adjourned [Play resumed 21/2/63]} Qd7 {[Sealed
move] I now decided that if I was going to win I would have to break up his
constant threats on my f-Pawn h-Pawn and utilise my better Rooks and highly
superior Pawn structure. If now he exchanged off Queens then I place my Rooks
on probably f7 & e7 and bring my King up to the centre with every chance of a
win considering the relative Pawn positions. If he now moves the Queen to e4
my only chance seems to be forcing .....f5. However I must at the same time
keep the h-Pawn and e-Pawn defended. The only way to do this seemed the way
that occured in the game.} 41. Qe4 Qe7 42. Rb3 {This is a line which I hoped
he would play. It seems to offer him even winning prospects.} ({The more
critical line which I examined was now...} 42. Kd1 {(or some indifferent move)}
f5 43. gxf5 Rxf5 44. Rxh7+ Qxh7 45. Rxh7+ Kxh7 {But this seems to be all in
Black's favour and White has little hope of survival. However the best move
which I found in looking over the game afterwards was 42.Rf3! However White
can hardly be blamed for playing the tempting 42.Rb3}) (42. Rf3 $1) 42... f5
43. gxf5 Rxf5 44. Rb7 {If White can now gain one free move he must win.
However.........} Rxf2+ 45. Kd1 Rf1+ 46. Kd2 R8f2+ 47. Kd3 Qxa3+ 48. Rb3 Rf3+ {
This and the next move are what White had missed.} 49. Kc4 Rc3+ 50. Rxc3 Qxc3+
51. Kd5 {Up to here as in my adjournment analysis. I now worked out a neat
finish.} Qa5+ 52. Kc4 (52. Ke6 Qa6+) (52. Kd6 Qa6+ (52... Qd8+ 53. Kc5 (53. Ke6
Qb6+) (53. Kxe5 {seems reasonable.}) 53... Qf8+) 53. Qc6 Qxc6+ 54. Kxc6 Rc1 {
White is obliged to resign. The game is quicker.}) 52... Qa4+ 53. Kd3 (53. Kd5
Qb5+ {Forces the above variation}) 53... Qb5+ $1 54. Kd2 Qa5+ 55. Kd3 {[114']}
Qc3+ {[112'] I now lead the tournament with 4/4} 0-1

