1. Nf3 d5 2. g3 c5 3. Bg2 Nc6 4. O-O e5 5. d3 g6 6. Nbd2 Bg7 7. c4 d4 8. Ne4 {
I doubt the wisdom of this procedure as 8.....f5 gains a tempo.} Qe7 9. a3 h6 {
To prevent the Bishop or Knight going to g5.} ({Turner suggested as stronger...
.} 9... f5 $1 10. Bg5 $2 Qf8 $1 {etc.}) 10. Qa4 f5 11. Nh4 $6 {Sending the
game into a turmoil.} fxe4 {On the assumption that my minor pieces will have
more scope than his and that my control of the centre will help.} (11... Kf7
12. Nd2 Z0 13. Bd5+ {doesn't look happy.}) 12. Nxg6 Qd6 13. Bxe4 Bd7 ({If.....
} 13... Nf6 {both of} 14. Nxh8 (14. Bxc6+) 14... Bxh8 15. Bg6+ {look good for
him}) 14. Nxh8 Bxh8 15. f4 {by far the best} Nge7 16. Qd1 O-O-O 17. fxe5 ({
I expected here, or the last move, .....} 17. f5) 17... Bxe5 18. Bf4 Bf5 $1 {
Giving up his best Bishop and leaving Black access to e3.} 19. Bxc6 ({if..} 19.
Bxf5+ Nxf5 20. Z0 Ne3) 19... Nxc6 20. Qd2 (20. Bxh6 $2 Bxg3 {and White is in
trouble}) 20... Bg4 {Very strong. His weak e2 & e3 cause trouble.} 21. Rae1 Re8
{Here he offered a draw. This would have kept me in the lead with 4.5 to his 4
with no-one else nearer than 3.5 . Therefore as I had already played the next
two strongest players ( Harding & Groves) I agreed. This was the last round
and I won with 4.5/5 2nd T.Turner 4/5 3rd C.Harding 3.5/5} 0-1

