{Time control 40 moves in 2 hrs} 1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 g6 3. Nc3 d5 4. cxd5 Nxd5 5.
g3 Bg7 6. Bg2 c6 7. e3 {White adopts a solid if non-aggressive position.} O-O {
[7']} 8. Nge2 Nxc3 {To equalise Black must play .....e5 or .....c5} ({If
immediately} 8... e5 9. Nxd5 cxd5 10. dxe5 {the d-Pawn is en prise.}) 9. bxc3 {
Leaving the Knight at a loose end but opening the b-file and allowing the
Bishop to be developed at a3.} e5 10. d5 {Black has an easy game after the
alternatives.} (10. dxe5) (10. O-O) 10... cxd5 11. Bxd5 Nc6 12. Qb3 {
Threatening 13.Ba3} Qc7 (12... Na5 {is much too risky.}) 13. Ba3 {[41']} Re8 {
[39']} 14. e4 {[50']} Be6 {[43'] Now I have quite a good game.} 15. O-O Rac8
16. Rab1 {[58']} b6 {[44']} 17. Qb5 {[74'] A definite plan for White was not
too easy to find.} Bd7 {[53']} 18. Qc4 (18. Z0 {The threat is...} Nd4 19. Qd3
Nxe2+ 20. Qxe2 Qxc3) 18... Be6 19. Rfd1 Nd4 $3 {A fine move winning material.}
20. Qxc7 (20. Bxe6 fxe6 (20... Rxe6 $2 21. Qxc7 Nxe2+ 22. Kf1 Nxg3+ 23. hxg3 {
if...} Rxc7 24. Rd8+) 21. Qxc7 {etc.}) (20. cxd4 Qxc4 21. Bxc4 Bxc4) (20. Nxd4
Qxc4 (20... exd4 21. Qxc7 Rxc7 22. Bxe6 dxc3 $1 (22... Rxc3)) 21. Bxc4 Rxc4 {
etc.}) 20... Nxe2+ 21. Kf1 Nxg3+ 22. hxg3 Bh3+ (22... Rxc7 23. Bxe6 {I have to
reply...} fxe6 (23... Rxe6 24. Rd8+)) 23. Ke1 Rxc7 24. c4 {[90']} Bf8 {[68']
The Bishop exchange is favourable to Black.} 25. Rb3 Bd7 {Gains a tempo in
view of 26......Ba4} 26. Rdd3 Bxa3 27. Rxa3 Kg7 28. Rd2 Re7 29. Rad3 Bc8 30.
Ra3 f5 {Beginning the decisive KS advance.} 31. Re2 Kf6 32. f4 {Preventing 32..
...f4 after which my KS Pawns advance, but to no avail.} exf4 33. gxf4 fxe4 34.
Rae3 ({After} 34. Bxe4 {he loses the c-Pawn.}) 34... Bf5 {The e-Pawn is immune
as White's c-Pawn needs defending by the Bishop.} 35. a4 h5 36. Kf2 g5 37.
fxg5+ Kxg5 38. Bxe4 ({No better is...} 38. Rg3+ Bg4) 38... Rxc4 39. Bxf5 Rf4+
40. Kg3 Rxe3+ 41. Rxe3 Kxf5 42. a5 {A very good game. we got a total score of
4 so advanced to day 2. [BIUCA is the British and Irish Universities Chess
Association. John Saunders told me he believes the participation of Dublin
changed the name from BUCA to BIUCA A report is in BCM 1963 p141-2 (Sean
Parker)]} 0-1

