1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 d6 3. Nc3 Nbd7 4. e4 e5 5. Nge2 Be7 6. g3 c6 7. Bg2 O-O 8. O-O
Re8 9. h3 Bf8 10. Kh2 {so as to play f4 which isn't so good straight away with
the King on the g1-a7 diagonal.} a5 11. Be3 Rb8 {Black's counterplay on the Qs
is not too promising as the situation in the centre isn't yet settled.} 12. a4
b6 13. f4 Ba6 14. b3 b5 $2 {This simple logical advance is doomed to failure
since Black's position is not strong enough to warrant it. His a-Pawn is now
at a loose end.} 15. axb5 cxb5 16. fxe5 $1 {Winning the a-Pawn in conjunction
with the next two moves & the best one.} dxe5 17. dxe5 Nxe5 (17... Rxe5 $4 18.
Bf4) 18. Qxd8 Rbxd8 19. Rxa5 b4 {The best chance.} 20. Nd5 $6 {Plunging into a
deluge of complications.} ({The alternative was....} 20. Rxa6 bxc3 21. Bd4 Rb8
22. Rb6) 20... Nxd5 {[42']} 21. Bd4 ({Also playable was...} 21. exd5 Nxc4 22.
bxc4 Rxe3 23. Rxa6 Rxe2) 21... Nxc4 $1 {[80'] After 38 minutes thought and
best. The position was very complicated.} ({e.g.} 21... Nc6 22. Rxd5 $1 ({
Not...} 22. Rxa6 $2 Nxd4 23. Nxd4 Ne3 $3)) (21... Bxc4 $2 22. bxc4 Nxc4 23.
Rxd5) 22. Rxa6 Nde3 23. Rc1 $1 {This results in a strong passed c-Pawn.} Nxg2
24. bxc4 Rxe4 25. Kxg2 Rxe2+ 26. Kf3 Rh2 {Underestimating my c-Pawn.} 27. Be3
Rxh3 28. c5 Rh5 29. Rb6 Rf5+ 30. Ke4 Rdd5 $2 {A time-trouble error the Rook
was needed on the back rank.} 31. Bd4 $1 f6 ({Not....} 31... Bxc5 $4 32. Rxc5
Rxc5 33. Rb8+ Rc8 34. Rxc8#) 32. c6 Bc5 {He must lose a piece.} 33. c7 Kf7 $1
34. Rxb4 $1 ({If now....} 34. c8=Q $4 Rxd4+ 35. Kxf5 (35. Ke3 Rd8+ 36. Ke2 Rxc8
) 35... g6#) 34... Bxd4 35. Rxd4 Rfe5+ 36. Kd3 Rc5 37. c8=Q Rxc8 38. Rxc8 Rg5 {
I now expected to win with consumate ease and played on very quickly and
carelessly.} 39. Rc7+ Ke6 40. g4 h6 41. Rcd7 g6 42. R4d6+ Ke5 43. Ke3 $2 {
Whilst this doesn't give away the win, there was no justification for leaving
the g-Pawn.} ({Easiest was.....} 43. Rd5+ Ke6 (43... Kf4 44. Rxg5) 44. Rxg5)
43... Rxg4 44. Rd5+ Ke6 45. R7d6+ Ke7 46. Ra6 f5 47. Rb5 Re4+ 48. Kf3 Re6 49.
Rbb6 ({Easiest was...} 49. Rb7+ Kf6 50. Rxe6+ Kxe6 51. Rh7 h5 52. Kf4) 49...
Rxb6 50. Rxb6 Kf7 51. Kf4 Kg7 52. Ke5 h5 53. Rb7+ $4 {Whether I can win after
this is doubtful although at the time I was still under the impression that
anything would win.} (53. Kf4 $1 Kh6 54. Ra6 h4 55. Rb6 Kh5 56. Rb8 $1 g5+ {
or else 57.Rh8+} 57. Kxf5 {Threatens mate and wins both Pawns.}) 53... Kh6 54.
Kf6 {This only makes things worse.} (54. Kf4 $142) 54... h4 {About here I
began to realise what was going on, rather too late unfortunately.} 55. Rb4 Kh5
56. Ke5 h3 57. Kf4 g5+ 58. Kg3 (58. Kxf5 {Loses.}) 58... g4 59. Rb2 Kg5 60. Rf2
Kf6 61. Kf4 Kg6 62. Ra2 Kh6 63. Ra6+ Kh7 64. Kg3 Kg7 {He offered a draw here.}
65. Rb6 Kh7 $4 {In his turn Black thinks that anything will do.} (65... Kf7 {
Draws easily.}) 66. Rf6 f4+ 67. Rxf4 {Icould still lose by 67.Kxf4 or 57.Kxg4
Rather a comedy of errors, both players completely undersetimating their
opponents resources. Quite an interesting game though. In Rd4 I had to agree
to a draw with R.C.Vaughan as circumstances made it impossible for me to play
him.} 1-0

