1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 g6 3. Nc3 d5 4. Nf3 Bg7 5. Qb3 c6 {According to M.C.O. this is
playable but unenterprising.} 6. cxd5 Nxd5 ({According to M.C.O. 9th Edn,
better is...} 6... cxd5 7. Bg5 e6 8. e3 O-O 9. Bd3 Nc6 {etc.}) 7. e4 Nb6 {
M.C.O. gives no alternatives to this.} 8. Be3 Be6 ({M.C.O. gives...} 8... O-O {
as better.}) 9. Qc2 ({According to M.C.O. after} 9. d5 cxd5 10. exd5 Nxd5 11.
Rd1 {White stands slightly better. I prefer 9.Qc2}) 9... O-O 10. b3 {I have no
fear of 10......Nc4 or 11......Bc4 but as I have time I restrict Black's
pieces.} N8d7 11. Be2 Bg4 12. O-O e5 {This may well be premature.} 13. dxe5
Bxf3 {Clearly necessary before capture of the e-Pawn.} 14. Bxf3 Nxe5 15. Be2
Qe7 {Guarding c5} 16. f4 Ned7 17. Bf3 Nc5 (17... Nd5 $6 {isn't too good after..
.} 18. Nxd5 cxd5 19. e5) 18. Rac1 {sacrifices the e-Pawn but White is left
with two Bishops against two Knights and better Rooks.} (18. Nd5 $5 cxd5 19.
Bxc5 Qc7 $3) 18... Bxc3 19. Qxc3 Nxe4 20. Qe5 $1 {Against anything else ......
Re8 or ......f5 is good.} Qxe5 21. fxe5 f5 ({of course not...} 21... Rfe8 $2
22. Bxe4 Rxe5 23. Bxb6) 22. Rfe1 ({When playing 20.Qe5! I had intended a
variation something like.} 22. Bxe4 fxe4 23. Rxf8+ Rxf8 24. Bxb6 axb6 25. Re1 {
but after...} Re8 26. Rxe4 {The Black King advances I probably lose the e-Pawn
although the game would probably be drawn.}) 22... Nd5 23. Bh6 $1 {Better than
23.Bd4 or 23.Bc5} Rfe8 24. Bxe4 fxe4 25. Rxe4 Re6 {Forced Black was in time
trouble here.} 26. g3 Rae8 {This loses against White's neat reply.} ({Correct
was....} 26... Nf6 $3) 27. Rf1 $1 R8e7 {Black prefers the quick way out but he
had no reply. White threatens to double on the f-file followed by check on f8.}
(27... Nc3 28. Re3 Nxa2 29. Ref3) (27... Nf6 28. exf6 Rxe4 29. f7+) (27... a6
28. Re2 Z0 29. Ref2 Z0 30. Rf8+) ({The best try} 27... g5 28. Bxg5 Rxe5 29. Rg4
Kh8 30. Bh6 R5e7 31. Bf8 Rd7 32. Ba3 $1 Z0 33. Bb2+) 28. Rf8# {2 A good game.
Hayes won the match 6 -} 1-0

