1. Nh3 e5 2. g3 d5 3. Bg2 f5 {White intends a Kings Indian set-up in which his
Knight (h3) is not in the way of his f-Pawn.} 4. d3 Nf6 5. c4 ({Possibly
better was...} 5. Bg5 {followed by...} Z0 6. e4) 5... Nc6 6. Nc3 Be6 7. Ng5 Bg8
{If White considered that Black was inconvenienced by this manoeuvre he was
mistaken. In fact White wastes a lot of time with this Knight.} 8. Qb3 {
In view of Black's next move this doesn't turn out too well.} Nd4 $1 9. Qa4+ (
9. Qxb7 Rb8 10. Qxa7 Nc2+) 9... Qd7 10. Nb5 Nxb5 11. cxb5 {White gives up a
Pawn in order to keep Queens on , to secure the open file and keep some play
against my centre Pawns.} a6 12. Bd2 Qxb5 13. Qc2 Bd6 14. O-O h6 15. Nf3 d4 $1
{White is denied d4.} (15... Be6) 16. Nh4 Be6 17. Ng6 Rg8 18. a4 Qb6 19. e4 {
This has the disadvantage of closing the long White diagonal.} fxe4 20. dxe4 {
[33']} Qb3 {[40']} (20... O-O-O 21. b4 {with a QS attack}) 21. Qc1 Bc4 {
so after f5 he will not have the Rook behind the Pawn.} 22. Re1 ({bad would be.
...} 22. Rd1 Be2 23. Re1 Bf3) 22... Bf7 23. Nh4 Qe6 24. f4 O-O-O 25. fxe5 Bxe5
26. Nf3 g5 {To prevent 27.Bf4} 27. Qc5 Nd7 28. Qa7 Qd6 29. Rac1 Rg6 $1 30. Ba5
{Apparently very strong but it results only in the win of a Pawn and White's
pieces are driven back.} b6 31. Qxa6+ Kb8 32. Bd2 Nc5 33. Qf1 Rf6 34. Bb4 {
White's only hope} d3 $1 ({if...} 34... g4 35. Bxc5 bxc5 36. Qb5+) 35. Bxc5
bxc5 36. Red1 g4 37. Qe1 gxf3 {At last the much travelled Knight falls. An
interesting game .Black holds his own in the early parts of the game by
various tactical points, White starta a rather ragged attack but is driven
back with a lost game.} 0-1

