1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bb5 a6 4. Ba4 Nf6 5. O-O Be7 6. Re1 b5 7. Bb3 d6 8. c3
O-O 9. h3 Na5 10. Bc2 c5 11. d4 Qc7 12. Nbd2 Bd7 13. Nf1 Rfe8 14. Ne3 g6 15.
Bd2 Bf8 16. dxc5 dxc5 17. Nh2 Be6 18. Qf3 Rad8 19. Rad1 Bg7 20. Bb1 {Fairly
standard Ruy Lopez manoeuvering up to here. Now Black tries to stem White's KS
attack to some extent whilst retaining the better of it on the QS.} h5 21. g4
c4 {Fixing White's QS and controlling d3 so as to play ......Nb7-c5-d3 at ome
stage.} ({Black has a choice between the variation played and....} 21... hxg4
22. Nhxg4 Nxg4 23. Nxg4 Bxg4 24. Qxg4 {or 24.hxB} Nc4 25. Bc1) 22. Qg3 {
I was worried White may be able to play 23.Nf5...... therefore} Kh7 23. Ng2 Qc8
{Leaving White embarassed for a good continuation.} 24. g5 {Releases all the
tension and blocks the KS. Also it leaves his h-Pawn weak.} Nd7 25. f4 exf4 26.
Bxf4 Nc6 (26... Nc6 {to answer} 27. Bc7 {with} Be5 28. Bxe5 Ndxe5 {and then
say......} 29. Z0 Qb7 30. Z0 {and} b4 {with a QS initiative.}) 27. Nf3 Nc5 {
Relieving the threat of 28.Bc7} 28. Be3 $2 {White attempts to stem my coming
QS advance but loses a pawn. His position was becoming difficult in any case.}
Na4 ({Also good is} 28... Nd3 29. Bxd3 cxd3 {winning a Pawn.}) 29. Rxd8 Rxd8
30. Bc1 Bxh3 31. Nf4 Be6 32. Qh4 {White wanted to terminate his games, so he
offered to resign this game if I agreed to resign the other. I agreed.} 0-1

