1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 g6 3. Nc3 d5 4. Nf3 Bg7 5. e3 O-O 6. Bd3 ({More vigerous is} 6.
Qb3 dxc4 7. Bxc4 Nfd7 8. O-O Nb6 9. Be2 Be6 10. Qc2 Nc6 11. Rd1 Bc4 12. Bxc4
Nxc4 $11) 6... b6 7. e4 {[19'] White's position hardly merits this energetic
advance. He castle instead. Now his centre becomes weak and Black's pieces
find good squares.} dxe4 {[7']} 8. Nxe4 Bb7 9. Nxf6+ Bxf6 10. d5 {Now his
centre is very shaky.} Re8 11. h4 $6 {White apparently though he had a strong
enough centre to warrant a KS attack. However with his King in the centre ,
this is doomed to failure.} e6 12. Bg5 $2 {This is a downright overestimation
of his chances. If White thought I was waek on the KS he is soon disillusioned.
} exd5+ 13. Kf1 ({Thre is nothing better e.g.} 13. Be3 d4) 13... dxc4 {This is
cleaner.} ({I considered both....} 13... d4) ({and} 13... Nd7) 14. Bxf6 $2 ({
Hoping to keep some of the pieces on but better was the variation I expected...
} 14. Bxc4 Qxd1+ 15. Rxd1 Bxf3 16. gxf3 Bxg5 17. hxg5 Nc6 18. Bb5 Re6 {when I
am faced with much stiffer problems.}) 14... Qxf6 (14... Qxd3+ {Is playable
but unclear. After 14......Qxd3+ I get a clearly won endgame due to the badly
placed White pieces.}) 15. Bxc4 Ba6 $1 {Putting an end to any hopes of
salvation by White. His Bishop goes and Black develops all his pieces quickly.}
16. Bxa6 Nxa6 17. Qc2 c5 18. Re1 Nb4 19. Rxe8+ Rxe8 20. Qb1 (20. Qa4 Qe7) 20...
Qe6 21. h5 {No better or worse than anything else.} Qe2+ 22. Kg1 Nd3 23. Qf1
Qxf1+ (23... Nxf2 {is interesting}) 24. Kxf1 Nxb2 25. h6 Nd3 {Immobilising the
White Knight and threatening to Queen the c-Pawn.} 26. Rh4 {At last the Rook
emereges.} b5 27. g3 f6 0-1

