1. e4 Nf6 2. e5 Nd5 3. Nf3 d6 4. Bc4 Nb6 5. Bb3 Nc6 6. d3 {White can soundly
offer his e-Pawn here as Black, in taking it ,leaves himself behind in
development and open to attack. However, a somewhat better way would have been
Castling.} Nxe5 7. Be3 (7. d4 {seemed better as White would be well placed
after 7......Nb6 or....} Nxf3+ 8. Qxf3) ({Alternatively..} 7. Nxe5 dxe5 8. f4)
7... g6 (7... Bg4 $4 8. Nxe5 {winning a piece.}) 8. Nxe5 dxe5 9. Nd2 Bg7 10.
Ne4 {White would still be well placed if he began a KS attack.} O-O 11. O-O Nd5
{A neat move. The White KBishop's diagonal is closed. 12.f4 is prevented and ..
....Be6 exchanging off Bishops is threatened.} 12. c3 {To close the long
diagonal} (12. Qf3 {looked good. The threat being 13.BxN!}) 12... b6 {Possibly
threatening ......Ba6 either beforee or after ......NxB. Also prevents N or B
to c5.} 13. Qd2 Be6 14. Ng5 $2 {Giving up any positional advantage he had.
Black now simplifies and comes out with an extra Pawn and a better position.} (
{Correct was....} 14. Bg5 {exerting pressure on White's position.}) 14... Nxe3
15. fxe3 Bxb3 16. axb3 h6 17. Nf3 c5 {Preventing d4 which would get rid of
Blacks doubled Pawns but would leave White with doubled isolated ones.} 18. e4
{To prevent 18.....e5 or 18.....f5} Qd7 19. c4 {This move can hardly be
justified as the hole on d4 can't be erased.} a5 {Stabilising the QS.} 20. Qc2
Rad8 21. Rad1 Qd6 {With the QSimmobile and the centre under control Black can
begin an assault on the KS which White can't do much about.} 22. Ne1 {Hoping
to get the Knight to d5 or b5.} f5 {Black soon takes over the KS as well.} 23.
Nf3 ({White can never reply....} 23. exf5 {because of...} e4) 23... g5 24. h3
Bf6 {To play ....h5 followed by ....g4} 25. Nh2 f4 26. Ng4 Bg7 27. Nh2 {
White can do nothing but wait for the attack.} h5 28. Kh1 Qg6 29. Nf3 g4 30.
hxg4 hxg4 31. Nh2 Qh5 32. Kg1 Rd6 33. Qd2 Rh6 34. g3 fxg3 35. Rxf8+ Bxf8 0-1

