1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 e6 3. Nc3 Bb4 4. e3 Bxc3+ {There doesn't seem to be any reason
to play this without provocation.} 5. bxc3 c5 6. Bd3 Nc6 7. Nf3 d6 8. O-O b6 9.
d5 $6 Na5 {Black's play is safe.} (9... exd5 10. cxd5 Nxd5 11. Bb5 Nde7 12. Ng5
{Threat 15.Qh5 or 13.Qf3} Be6 {Seems fairly good for Black although he is
somewhat tied up.}) 10. e4 e5 {Now he gets a cramped position.} 11. h3 {
Covers g4 and allows the following Knight manoeuvre,} Ba6 {As I can
satisfactorly defend the c-Pawn the Knight and Bishop are rather out of play.}
12. Qe2 O-O 13. Nh2 Qe8 14. Bg5 Nd7 {He can hardly allow 15.BxN} 15. Ng4 f6 16.
Bd2 Rf7 17. Ne3 g6 {To prevent 18.Nf5} 18. Ng4 Kh8 19. g3 (19. f4 {Black can
play his Knight to e5 after exchanging. I therefore want to recapture with a
Pawn.}) 19... Re7 {A move which is stronger than it looks. Black now threatens
20......f5 even if I play f4.} 20. Nh6 {Prevents 20......f5.} Qf8 21. g4 Rae8
22. h4 Bc8 23. h5 Nb8 24. hxg6 hxg6 25. f3 {This may be where I went wrong.} (
25. f4 {isn't playable as my e-Pawn is too weak.}) (25. g5 {Seems good. I was
playing fairly quickly at this stage as my opponent was in time trouble, and I
didn't want to give him too much time to think. Had I have taken my time I
might have found 25.g5}) 25... Rh7 26. Rf2 g5 27. Nf5 Bxf5 28. exf5 $2 ({
In view of the final position} 28. gxf5 {seemed better.}) 28... Ree7 29. Rh2
Rxh2 30. Qxh2+ Rh7 31. Qg2 Qh6 32. Kf2 Qh4+ 33. Qg3 Qh2+ 34. Qg2 Qh4+ 35. Qg3
Qh2+ 36. Qg2 {I couldn't find anything better for White.} Nd7 {The game had to
end here due to time regulations. White has an extremely difficult game. Black
threatend ......e5!! followed by ......Ne5 and White loses in all variations.}
(36... Nd7 37. Rg1 $2 (37. Qxh2 Rxh2+ 38. Ke3 e4 $3 39. fxe4 {forced.} Ne5 {
40.Resigns}) ({However White seems to get the draw by giving up the c-Pawn to
prevent ......e5} 37. Be4 $1 Nxc4 38. Bc1 $1 {Now how is Black to make
progress? If he opens up the QS White's Rook gets good play and ensures the
draw. If he doesn't how does the Knight on d7 get into the game?} b5 39. Rb1 a6
40. a4 bxa4 41. Bd3 Ndb6 42. Ra1 {with a probable draw.}) 37... e4 $1 38. fxe4
(38. Bxe4 Nxc4 39. Bc1 Nde5 {and White a Pawn down is tied up.} ({Even better
seems.} 39... Qe5 $1)) 38... Ne5 39. Be2 Nexc4 $1 40. Bc1 Qe5 $1 41. Qg3 Rh2+ {
There doesn't seem anything better for White here.}) 1/2-1/2

