1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 e6 3. Nc3 c5 4. d5 exd5 5. cxd5 g6 6. e4 d6 7. f4 Bg7 8. Bb5+
Nfd7 9. Bd3 O-O 10. Nf3 Re8 11. O-O Na6 12. Nd2 Nc7 13. a4 Nf6 14. h3 b6 $2 (
14... Rb8 $142) 15. Qf3 {As played by Taimanov v Trifunovic in nearly 
(essentially) the same position. Kmoch quotes the game in his "Pawn power in
Chess". He says that Black is in a pretty hopeless position and considers only
......Rb8. Stephenson finds a move that the two grandmasters missed.} Nfxd5 $1
16. Nxd5 (16. exd5 Bd4+ 17. Kh1 Re3 18. Qd1 Rxd3 $19) 16... Nxd5 17. Bb5 {
There seems nothing better than this.} Ne3 ({He also wins easily by...} 17...
Bd4+) 18. e5 {The only hope.} Nxf1 19. Qxa8 ({Not} 19. Nxf1 Bd7 $3) (19. Bxe8 {
is no good either}) 19... Nxd2 20. Qd5 {Anything loses.} (20. Bxe8 Nb3 $1) (20.
Bxd2 Bd7 $1) 20... dxe5 21. Qxd2 Qxd2 22. Bxd2 Rd8 23. Be3 exf4 24. Bxf4 Bxb2
25. Ra2 Bf6 26. a5 Be6 27. Rc2 bxa5 {The games was published in "Chess" I have
the consolation of having made a contribution to opening theory, even if I was
at the wrong end.} 0-1

