1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bb5 a6 4. Ba4 Nf6 5. O-O b5 6. Bb3 Be7 7. Re1 d6 8. c3
O-O 9. h3 Na5 10. Bc2 c5 11. d4 Bb7 {As introduced in the 1958 U.S.S.R. Ch
1958.} 12. Nbd2 ({Instead of defending the e-Pawn Black counterattacks
tactically. It is justified by.....} 12. dxe5 dxe5 13. Qxd8 Raxd8 14. Nxe5 Nxe4
$1 15. Bxe4 Bxe4 16. Rxe4 Rd1+) (12. b4 {Tal v Geller U.S.S.R.1958}) 12... cxd4
13. cxd4 exd4 14. Nxd4 {I was willing to weaken my d-Pawn to get strong
squares for my pieces.} g6 {Preventing 15.Nf5 so I can play 15......Nd7
followed by ....Bf6} 15. Nf1 Nd7 16. Bh6 Re8 17. Ng3 Bf6 {[17']} 18. a4 {
[59'] White's best plan was to probably attack on the KS.} Qb6 19. Nf3 {
He is more or less obliged to give up the b-Pawn but he retains good attacking
prospects as my KS is somewhat open.} Bxb2 {[27']} 20. Be3 {[75']} ({If} 20.
Rb1 Bc3 21. Z0 {then....} b4) 20... Qc6 21. Nd4 Qc7 22. axb5 $6 {Scarificing
the exchange for the attack. However, he was short of time so a more
conservative policy was in order.} Bxa1 23. Qxa1 Nc4 24. b6 Ndxb6 {Opening the
2nd rank so as to be able to defend g7 by playing ......f6.} 25. Bh6 d5 $1 {
The best defence.} 26. f4 {White was now in very bad time trouble and misses a
neat simplifying line. However, he doesn't seem to have any good move.} Qc5 $1
27. Kh1 Qa3 28. Qxa3 Nxa3 29. Bd3 {Here we were both in bad time trouble and
the play was rushed.} dxe4 30. Nxe4 ({Not} 30. Bxe4 f5) 30... Bxe4 31. Rxe4
Red8 {Threat 32......f5} 32. Be2 Nd5 33. Nc6 Re8 $2 {In the time rush I lose
the a-Pawn.} 34. Rxe8+ Rxe8 35. Bxa6 Re6 36. Bb7 Re1+ 37. Kh2 {We have both
survived the time scramble & White has good drawing chances.} Rf1 38. Ba6 Rf2
39. Kg3 Rb2 40. Nd4 Ne3 41. f5 Nxf5+ 42. Nxf5 gxf5 43. Bc8 Rb6 44. Bf4 {
22 Adjudicated. Win for Black. Sussex won the match 28 -} (44. Bf4 {best seems}
Rg6+ ({or} 44... Rb5) 45. Kf3 Nb5 $1 {defending the f-Pawn} 46. Be5 Nd6 47. Bd7
{Then what?}) 0-1

