1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 e6 3. Nc3 Bb4 4. e3 c5 5. Nf3 O-O 6. Bd3 d5 7. O-O b6 8. a3
cxd4 9. Nxd5 $1 exd5 (9... Nxd5 10. cxd5) 10. axb4 dxc4 11. Bxc4 dxe3 12. Bxe3
{White's advantage cosists of his superior development and the great activity
of his two Bishops on the open board.The fact he has doubled isolated Pawns is
of no consequence whatsoever.} Bb7 13. Qxd8 Rxd8 14. Ne5 ({Not} 14. Rxa7 $2
Rxa7 15. Bxb6 Nc6 $1) 14... Rf8 {Forced.} (14... Nd5 {is answered by} 15. Rfd1
{and Black can't develop is b-Knight.}) 15. Rfd1 {Threatening 16.Nxf7} Nc6 16.
Nd7 Nxd7 ({Black doesn't want his Pawns doubled, but even......} 16... Rfd8 {
was better although still not good e.g.} 17. Nxf6+ gxf6 18. Bd4) 17. Rxd7 Nd8 (
{I expected} 17... Ne5 18. Rxb7 Nxc4 19. Bd4 {Although this has the
disadvantage for Black that his Knight may become stranded. However it offered
better chances.}) 18. Bg5 {Not giving Black a moment to breathe.} Bc8 $4 {
Black had no satisfactory move but this just sealed his fate. A faint glow of
hope was offered by sacrificing the a-pawn by either of.........} (18... Bc6) (
18... Rc8) 19. Rxd8 Rxd8 20. Bxd8 Be6 21. Bxe6 Rxd8 {Black could have spared
himself the rest but probably didn't want to resign whilst the rest of his
team were still playing their openings.} 22. Bc4 Rd4 23. Rxa7 Rd1+ 24. Bf1 g6
25. Rb7 Rb1 26. Rxb6 Rxb2 27. b5 h5 28. g3 Rb4 29. Rb8+ Kg7 30. b6 h4 31. Kg2
g5 32. b7 f5 {[38']} 33. gxh4 {[27']} gxh4 34. Ba6 Rb6 35. h3 f4 36. Re8 f3+
37. Kh2 Rb2 38. b8=Q Rxf2+ 39. Kg1 Rg2+ 40. Kf1 {1 [29'] Southampton Uni won
the match 5 -} 1-0

