1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 g6 3. Nc3 d5 4. Nf3 Bg7 5. Bf4 c6 6. e3 O-O 7. Be2 Nbd7 8. h3 {
Gives the Bishop a retreat.} dxc4 9. Bxc4 Nb6 10. Bb3 (10. Bd3 {is answered by.
.} Nbd5 ({or} 10... Be6) 11. Z0 Nb4 {which is a nuisance.}) 10... Nbd5 11. Nxd5
Nxd5 12. Bg3 {There is no necessity to go to h2.} Qa5+ 13. Qd2 Qxd2+ ({Black
would have gained nothing by trying to prevent me castling by...} 13... Qa6)
14. Nxd2 b6 {So as to play ......Ba6 and support an eventual ......c5} 15. Bc4
Bb7 16. O-O Rac8 {Threatening 17....c5} 17. Bxd5 {About best.} cxd5 18. Rfc1
Rxc1+ {If Black doesn't exchange I play 19.Bc7 then double on the c-file.} 19.
Rxc1 Rc8 20. Rxc8+ {This gives me the much better end-game.} Bxc8 21. Bb8 {
I force his Pawns onto a6 & b5 giving my Knight a strong post at c5 and making
his Bishop pretty useless.} a6 22. Bc7 b5 23. Nb3 e6 24. Nc5 Kf8 25. Kf1 {
White threatens to march the King to a5 to win the Pawn.} Ke8 26. Ke2 Bf8 27.
Kd3 Bxc5 {He must play this sooner or later.} 28. dxc5 Kd7 29. Be5 {Preventing
......f6 & ......e5} Kc6 (29... a5 {Isn't so good as the QS reamains fluid as
now it is blocked.}) 30. b4 a5 {[59']} 31. a3 {[43']} axb4 32. axb4 Bb7 33. g4
{If I can get the g-Pawn to g5 I freeze his Pawns & my King will be able to
get through. However........} f5 $1 {Since an exchangeof Pawns will result in
his Bishop coming into play. I am obliged to play g5 when Black prevents
further penetration.} 34. g5 Kd7 35. Kd4 Bc6 36. f4 Ke7 37. Bf6+ Kd7 {
Berkshire won the match.} 1/2-1/2

