1. c4 e5 2. Nc3 Nf6 3. g3 d5 4. cxd5 Nxd5 5. Bg2 Nb6 6. d3 Be7 7. Be3 O-O 8.
Nh3 {A line played by Golombeck} c6 9. O-O Bg4 {As good a square as any for
the Bishop as the attack, or pin, on the e-Pawn may prove an embarassment
later in the game.} 10. f4 N8d7 11. fxe5 Nxe5 12. Qb3 Be6 13. Qc2 Nd5 $1 {
Neatly taking advantage of the peculiar placing of White's pieces.} 14. Nxd5 ({
If} 14. Bf2 Nb4 {causes White trouble.}) ({If} 14. Bd2 Bc5+ 15. Kh1 {and
Bishop or Knight to e3 is good. White's method loses him his KBishop for my
QBishop which leaves his QS weak.}) 14... Bxd5 15. Nf4 Bxg2 16. Kxg2 Ng4 17.
Bg1 ({If} 17. Bd2 {Black has threats like .........} Bb4 18. Z0 Qb6 19. Z0 Bxd2
20. Qxd2 Ne3+) 17... Bf6 18. h3 Ne5 ({If} 18... Nh6 19. g4) 19. d4 {Now the
e-Pawn is forever weak.} Ng6 20. Qc4 Qe7 21. Rae1 Nxf4+ 22. Rxf4 Bg5 23. Rf3
Qe4 {Now White is getting tied up. 24......Re8 & 24......Bd2 are threatened.}
24. Qd3 Rae8 25. Qxe4 Rxe4 26. e3 Rfe8 27. Bf2 h6 28. h4 Bf6 29. Rf4 R4e6 30.
Kf1 g5 31. Rf5 (31. hxg5 Bxg5 32. Rf3 {is very weak for White.}) 31... Kg7 32.
b3 Kg6 33. Ra5 $4 ({Tired of his passive position White tries to get some play
33.Ra5?? loses a Pawn Correct was.} 33. Rf3) 33... a6 34. b4 {Adjourned. White
has no good moves. Play resumed 12/1/65.} gxh4 {Sealed move.} 35. gxh4 h5 {
Threatening 36......Re4} 36. d5 {Best.} ({He must lose a Pawn. If} 36. Kg2 Bxd4
37. exd4 Rxe1 38. Bxe1 Rxe1 39. Rg5+ Kf6 $3 $19) 36... cxd5 37. Rxd5 Re4 38. a3
Bxh4 39. Rd7 R8e7 40. Rxe7 Bxe7 41. Rd1 Bg5 42. Ke2 b5 43. Kf3 ({If} 43. Rd6+
Re6 44. Rxe6+ fxe6 $19) 43... Re6 44. Rd4 Rc6 45. Rd8 $4 {2 Realising his
blunder immediately White resigned he was lost anyway. Black played well to
obtain a won position. University won the Match 3 -} 0-1

