1. e4 e6 2. d4 d5 3. Nc3 Bb4 4. e5 c5 5. Bd2 cxd4 6. Nb5 Bc5 7. b4 a6 {Now I
get an open a-file and White is left with a weak c-pawn.} 8. bxc5 axb5 9. Bxb5+
Bd7 10. a4 Qc7 11. Nf3 Qxc5 12. O-O Ne7 (12... Bxb5 13. axb5 Rxa1 14. Qxa1 Qxb5
{(14...Qxc2) is a bit much even in a simultaneous display.}) 13. Rb1 Nbc6 {
Prevents 14 Bb4} 14. Ne1 Qa7 ({Protects the b-pawn. If} 14... Nxe5 15. Bxd7+
Nxd7 16. Rxb7) 15. Nd3 Ng6 16. f4 Nge7 17. g4 g6 18. Qe1 {Hoping to benefit
from my weak black squares.} h5 19. f5 ({Initiating a rather wild sacrifice.} 
19. Qh4 O-O-O) 19... gxf5 20. gxf5 Nxf5 21. Rxf5 $6 exf5 22. Qh4 Qb8 23. Bg5
Qc7 ({Not} 23... Nxe5 $4 24. Nxe5 Qxe5 25. Re1) 24. Kh1 (24. Kf1 $142) 24...
Nd8 25. Bf6 $2 {Fatally forcing my rook onto the g-file.} Rg8 26. Qxd4 ({
She is lost anyway, but} 26. Bxd7+ Qxd7 27. Qxh5 Qxa4 {was better.}) 26... Bxb5
27. Rxb5 Qxc2 28. Ne1 {Forced.} Qe2 {Threatening 29......QxN and 29......Qf1+.
White can't stop this so she tries threatening mate herself.} 29. Qb4 Qf1# {
6 The simul was for prizewinners at the London Championships. She won 10, drew
4, Lost 6.} 0-1

