1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 g6 3. Nc3 d5 4. Bf4 Bg7 5. e3 a6 {Not a very good move
particularly in view of my 11th} 6. cxd5 Nxd5 7. Nxd5 Qxd5 8. Bxc7 Nc6 9. Ne2 {
This gets an excamation mark in "Indian Systems" by Pachman (Black having
played 5......0-0)} O-O ({More usual is} 9... Bg4 10. f3 Bxf3 $1) 10. Nc3 Qd7
11. Bb6 {A dominating post for the Bishop which shows the weakness of 5......
a6. Black is now completely stuck.} Re8 {Black's KS is immovable.} 12. Be2 e6 (
{If} 12... e5 13. d5) 13. Bf3 Ne7 14. Qb3 {Prevents 14......Nd5} Nf5 15. g3 {
To answer a future Qf6 with Bg2, also prevents 15......Nh5} Qe7 16. Rc1 {
Threatening 17.Ne4 followed by Rc7} Nd6 {Threatening to get some play after ...
..Bd7 now the b-Pawn is defended.} 17. Bc5 Qf6 18. Bg2 Bf8 19. O-O Qd8 20. Bb6
Qd7 21. a4 ({If} 21. Na4 {or 21.Ne2 threatening Rc7 Black can play.} Qb5) 21...
Be7 22. e4 Bd8 {Attacking my Bishop but too late.} 23. e5 Bxb6 24. Qxb6 Nf5 ({
If} 24... Nc4 25. Qb4 Z0 26. Ne4 Z0 27. Nd6) 25. Ne4 Qd8 ({And Not} 25... Qxd4
$4 26. Qxd4 Nxd4 27. Nf6+ Kf8 28. Nxe8 Ne2+ 29. Kh1 Nxc1 30. Nc7 $1) 26. Nf6+
Kh8 27. Rc7 {Very neat.} Rf8 {This loses straight off but equally hopeless was.
.....} ({.........} 27... Re7 28. Rxe7 $1 Qxe7 29. Rc1 Z0 30. Rc7 Qf8 (30...
Qd8 31. Bxb7) 31. Bxb7) 1-0

