1. e4 d6 2. d4 g6 3. f4 Bg7 4. Nf3 Nf6 5. Bd3 c5 6. c3 O-O 7. O-O cxd4 8. cxd4
d5 {[17']} 9. e5 {[8']} Nfd7 10. Nc3 Nb6 11. h4 Bg4 {Taking advantage of the
hole left by White's last move but this Bishop gets rather out on a limb.} 12.
Qe1 Nc6 13. Be3 Nb4 14. Bb1 Nc4 15. Bc1 {No real iconvenience this.} Nc6 16. b3
Bxf3 17. bxc4 Bg4 18. cxd5 {Best. White controls the centre and his minor
pieces are better placed} Nxd4 19. Qf2 Nf5 20. Be4 {Intending Rb1} Qa5 21. Bd2
Rac8 22. Rac1 (22. Rac1 {Preventing Black doubling Rooks e.g.} Qc5 (22... Rc7
23. d6 Rc6 24. Nd5) 23. Bxf5 Z0 24. Ne4) 22... b6 23. Kh2 {To prevent the
exchange of Queens by 23......Qc5. Also to be able to defend the h-Pawn by g3
if the Queen moves.} f6 $2 {Probably the losing move. Black's position was not
strong enough for this.} 24. d6 $1 exd6 25. Nd5 Qxa2 26. Rxc8 Rxc8 27. exf6 ({
Another way was} 27. Bxf5 Qxd5 28. Bxc8 (28. Bxg4 Rc2 {etc is ot so good.}))
27... Bf8 28. Bxf5 Bxf5 29. Qd4 {Threatening 30.Ra1 also 30.f7+} ({I had also
considered} 29. f7+ Kxf7 30. Nf6 {but Black plays} Qa4 (30... Kxf6 $4)) 29...
Kf7 (29... Qc4 $142) 30. Ra1 Rc4 31. Rxa2 Rxd4 32. Rxa7+ Ke8 33. f7+ Kd8 34.
Ra8+ Bc8 35. Nxb6 Rxd2 36. Rxc8+ Ke7 37. Rc7+ Ke6 38. Nd7 Ke7 {Ends it quickly}
(38... Kxf7 39. Nxf8+ Kxf8 40. Rxh7 {Wins for White}) 39. Nc5+ Kd8 40. Ne6# 1-0

