1. b4 d5 2. e3 e5 3. Bb2 e4 4. c4 c6 5. Qb3 Be6 {Black doesn't have too much
trouble against this type of opening. White's play is rather artificial. He
now makes 4 moves with his QKnight ending in the exchange of it!} 6. Na3 Nf6 7.
Nc2 Bd6 {Straightforward development.} 8. Nd4 Ng4 {I felt I was sufficiently
far ahead in development to play this. 9......Nxf2 is threatened.} 9. Nxe6 $2 {
Opening the f-file must be good for Black.} fxe6 10. Be2 Nxf2 $5 {Offering
good "Over the board" chances.} 11. Kxf2 ({If} 11. Bxg7 {Black has the choice
between.} Nxh1 ({and} 11... Rg8)) 11... Qh4+ 12. g3 Bxg3+ 13. Kg2 Bd6 14. Nh3
Nd7 {I want to play ......g5 (after ......o-o or ......Rg8) without the White
reply Bf6} 15. cxd5 exd5 16. Raf1 Rg8 17. Qd1 O-O-O ({If now} 17... g5 18. Bh5+
Kd8 19. Qg4) 18. Rf5 g6 19. Rf7 g5 20. Bg4 h5 21. Bxd7+ ({If now} 21. Bxh5 g4)
21... Rxd7 22. Rxd7 Kxd7 23. Bf6 Bxb4 24. Rf1 Bxd2 $1 {Intending to give up
the direct attack for enough Pawns.} 25. Qe2 Qg4+ $6 {Heading to an unclear
ending.} 26. Qxg4+ hxg4 27. Nxg5 Bxe3 28. Nf7 c5 29. Kg3 b5 30. Ne5+ Ke6 31.
Nxg4 d4 32. Rf5 $4 {An unfortunate mental lapse spoiling an interesting game.
White saw his mistake immediately.} Kxf5 {Prof R.J.H had 2/3 prior to this,
having beaten Barry & Wilkinson.} 0-1

