{Time limit 48 moves in 2hrs} 1. e4 e5 2. f4 exf4 3. Nf3 d6 4. Bc4 h6 5. b3 Nc6
({Rather better is} 5... Be7 {as I played against M.J.Owen a couple of years
ago.}) 6. Bb2 Nf6 7. Qe2 Be7 8. e5 {This being the last round and with nothing
at stake I intended to play a "do or die" sort of game.} dxe5 9. Nxe5 Nxe5 10.
Qxe5 O-O 11. Qxf4 {The position is a little precarious} (11. O-O Bd6) 11... Nd5
12. Bxd5 ({If now} 12. Qf3 {then} Bf6 {swaps a lot off. I therefore decided to
play for an attack and complications at the expense of the g-Pawn.}) 12... Qxd5
13. Nc3 $6 Qxg2 14. O-O-O {[25'] I now have good prospects against his King.
On the other hand his Bishops are very strong.} Bg5 {[30'] Giving back the
pawn for pressure on the d-Pawn.} 15. Qxc7 {[25']} Bf5 $1 {[57'] After much
thought and no doubt best.} 16. Kb1 {[37'] So that the d-Pawn won't be taken
with check.} Rac8 {Again best.} ({If} 16... Bxd2 17. Rhg1 Qxh2 18. Ne4 {etc.})
17. Qe5 Bxd2 18. Rhg1 {There may be nothing better than this, in which case
White's whole idea was overoptimistic, leading to an endgame a pawn down.} Bxc3
$1 {I had originally thought that this could simply be answered by 19.BxB} 19.
Qxf5 {Offers the h-Pawn to keep some attacking chances.} (19. Bxc3 Qxc2+ {
is answered by} 20. Ka1 {However} Qxc3+ {is check and can't be answered by
21Rxg7+ as I originally intended. What to do? Alternatives lead to endgames a
pawn down.}) (19. Rxg2) (19. Qxc3) 19... Qc6 (19... Qxh2 {allows me an
immediate draw by} 20. Bxc3 Rxc3 21. Rxg7+ {with perpetual check.}) 20. Bxc3
Qxc3 {[70']} 21. Rg3 {[70'] It looks very ominous with his 2 Pawn majority on
the KS.} Qc5 22. Qxc5 Rxc5 23. Rd7 Rfc8 24. c4 (24. Rxb7 {May possibly offer
more hope.}) 24... R8c7 25. Rgd3 Kh7 26. a4 f5 27. Kc2 f4 28. Kd2 Kg6 29. Ke2
Kh5 30. Kf3 g5 {help!} 31. R7d6 {Preventing 31.......Kh4} Rf7 32. h3 Rg7 33. h4
Rf5 {Black was playing fast wwith great confidence now.} 34. hxg5 hxg5 35. Rd7
g4+ 36. Ke4 Rff7 37. R7d5+ Kh4 ({Rather better is} 37... Rg5) 38. Rd1 {A
glimmer of hope appears.} Rh7 39. Rh1+ Kg3 40. Rg1+ Kf2 $4 {Giving me half a
point.} ({he can still win by} 40... Kh3 $1 41. Rh1+ (41. Rdd1 g3 $19) 41...
Kg2 $19) 41. Rdd1 {Never say die! I came equal with (amongst others) V.W.Knox
and J.E.Littlewood. [From CHESS October1973; The total prize money of £235
drew 134 competitors to Preston's first ever congress at Winckley Square
convent school. Entry fees of £290 meant prifit and new equipment for the
local club. 1st B.Ely 2-4 J.Horner, M.Lamping, A.Marsh]} 1/2-1/2

