{Time limit 40 moves in 2 hrs 30' then 16 moves per hour} 1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 e6 3.
Nf3 b6 4. g3 Bb7 5. Bg2 Be7 6. Nc3 O-O 7. O-O Ne4 8. Bd2 {I didn't know this
rather passive line.} f5 9. Qb3 ({Allows me to simplify quite favourably.} 9.
d5 {is an alternative.}) 9... Nxd2 10. Nxd2 Bxg2 11. Kxg2 c5 $5 {I'm not sure
about this. I end up with a weak d-Pawn on the open file but Block his c-Pawn
and get play for my Bishop.} 12. dxc5 ({If} 12. d5 e5) 12... Bxc5 13. Na4 Be7
14. Rac1 Na6 15. Nf3 Qc7 {My aim is an attack based on ......f4} 16. Qb5 Nc5
17. Nxc5 Bxc5 18. Rfd1 Rad8 {I would rather leave this Rook where it was but
didn't want to take the other of the f-file.} 19. Rd2 Qb7 {After much thought,
and best. White threatens to double Rooks and also play b4. My best is to pin
his Knight and also drive his Queen back.} 20. Rcd1 a6 21. Qa4 b5 22. cxb5 axb5
23. Qf4 ({I had expected.} 23. Qb3) 23... d5 {Leaves the e-Pawn weak but gives
me room.} 24. Rc2 Bd6 25. Qd4 f4 {Positive action is needed to counter his
play on the c-file.} 26. Rdc1 fxg3 27. hxg3 Qf7 $1 {Forcing him to give up the
c-file in view of the threat of ......e5 & .....e4 etc} 28. Rf1 Rc8 {I control
the c-file.} (28... Qf5 {isn't good enough. e.g.} 29. Rc6 Bb8 {and I probably
lose my b-Pawn.}) 29. Rxc8 Rxc8 30. Qb6 Qd7 (30... Qd7 {A continuation might be
} 31. Nd4 Rb8 32. Qc6 Qxc6 33. Nxc6 Rc8 34. Nd4 Be5 35. Nxe6 Bxb2 36. Rb1 Rc2 {
etc.}) 1/2-1/2

