{Time limit 40' for all moves} 1. e4 c5 2. Nf3 e6 3. d4 cxd4 4. Nxd4 Bc5 5. Nc3
Ne7 6. Bc4 {He was already taking more time than me, and this move doesn't
seem to useful.} a6 ({I considered briefly} 6... Qb6 7. Be3 Qxb2 {but it is
too complicated for this type of game. I decided to play solid moves quickly
and as a result he was 'behind' on time for the whole game.}) 7. O-O b5 8. Bb3
Bb7 9. Qd3 Qc7 10. Be3 d6 11. Rad1 Nd7 12. Kh1 Ne5 {Trying to win his e-pawn
but it's a bit risky.} 13. Qe2 b4 14. Ba4+ ({I was expecting} 14. Na4 {and was
wondering about} Bxe4 15. Nxc5 dxc5 16. Nxe6 {However, he replied
instantaneously with 14.Ba4+}) 14... Kf8 15. f4 Nd7 16. f5 {Suddenly he has a
strong innitiative. I didn't want to spend too long thinking so played it
"safe"} exf5 (16... e5 {Put both Knights en-prise but looked risky.}) 17. Nd5
Bxd5 18. exd5 Ne5 19. Nxf5 Nxf5 20. Rxf5 Bxe3 21. Qxe3 Ke7 {I felt that my
position would now hold together and that eventually my Knight could be a
better piece than the Bishop. Also I had a lot more time left than he had.} 22.
Qg5+ Kf8 23. Rdf1 h6 24. Qd2 a5 25. Qd4 f6 26. Rc1 Ke7 27. Rff1 Rhc8 28. Rfe1
Kf8 {I was playing solid moves quickly. I felt that the clock was my major
advantage. He was already rushing.} 29. Re3 Qc5 30. Rd1 Kf7 31. Bb3 Qxd4 32.
Rxd4 Ra7 (32... a4 $142) 33. a3 bxa3 34. bxa3 Rb8 35. a4 Rc7 36. h3 Rc5 37. Kg1
Rbc8 38. Kf2 Rc3 {White was now very short of time (~5') so I thought that I
could try to pressure him by a general advance on all fronts. However, time
was also short for me. I stopped recording the game. The remaining moves are
to the best of my recollection.} 39. Ke2 g5 40. g4 $2 {The mistake had to come
with time running out.} Rxe3+ 41. Kxe3 Rc3+ 42. Kf2 Rxh3 43. Bc4 {Trying to
activate his pieces.} Rh2+ {The rest of the game was a frantic rush although I
still had 4' left right at the end} 44. Ke3 Rxc2 45. Bb5 Rg2 46. Rd1 Rxg4 47.
Rc1 Rg3+ 48. Kf2 Nd3+ 49. Kxg3 Nxc1 50. Kg4 (50. Bc4 {Trapping the Knight
doesn't help.}) 50... Nb3 51. Kh5 Kg7 {I now advanced my KS pawns with the
help of the King and Knight, and queened the f-Pawn, White giving up his
Bishop for it to try to get a stalemate. We reached the following position. 
[White K h1, Pawns d, a4][Black Pawns h4, g3, d6, a5, King f4, Knight e3]
Deciding tht one could easily stalemate him in a time-rush. I took his d-Pawn
with the Knight moved the Knight out of the way and pushed the d-Pawn,
whereupon he resigned. A good result to beat the 1976 winner[Grade 185]. There
were now 7 players with 3/3} 0-1

