{Time limit 40' each for all moves} 1. e4 c5 2. f4 e6 3. Nf3 Nc6 4. Bb5 Nd4 {
I think that this surprised him.} 5. Nxd4 cxd4 6. c3 a6 7. Bd3 Bc5 8. Qh5 Qc7
9. e5 b5 {I felt that I had "won" the opening.} 10. b4 $2 {An oversight which
gains in importance when my b-Pawn eventually Queens.} Bxb4 11. Bb2 dxc3 12.
Nxc3 g6 13. Qe2 Ne7 14. Rc1 Bxc3 {This leaves me weak on the black squaresbut
his Knight was going to e4.} 15. Bxc3 Qb6 16. Bb4 Nd5 17. Bc5 Qb8 18. O-O f5 $5
{I felt that I had gone seriously wrong and something positive was needed.
However, I think this was probably wrong. White, however, was already behind
on time and decided to play it safe.} 19. Qf2 ({I think that} 19. exf6 {
may be the best e.g.} Nxf4 ({or} 19... Nxf6 20. f5 {I would probably have
played} Kf7) 20. f7+ $1) 19... Bb7 20. Qh4 Kf7 21. Be2 h6 22. Bf3 {Threatening
23.BxN and 24.Qe7+ or 24.Qf6+} Qd8 23. Qf2 Bc6 {Having secured some of the
weaknesses I thought that I might go on the offensive with ......g4. However,..
.....} 24. g4 $2 {Apart from being a mistake, this sort of thing is dangerous
when time is running out.} Nxf4 {He stretched out his hand to play 25.PxP??
but noticed the problem in time.} 25. Qg3 (25. gxf5 $4) 25... Qg5 $4 {One
can't expect to go 6 rounds without one horrendouserror. This is it. With the
clock ticking away I didn't want to risk .....Nd3 or .....BxB or....g4. I saw
my mistake immediately.} (25... Nd3) (25... Bxf3) (25... g5) 26. h4 Ne2+ {
The best chance as he was short of time.} 27. Bxe2 Qxd2 28. gxf5 exf5 ({Not} 
28... gxf5 $4 29. Bh5#) 29. Bh5 $4 {With no time to analyse he gets carried
away with visions of a mating attack.} (29. h5 {Threatening 30.Qxg6# would do.}
) 29... gxh5 {There is no choice. The rst of the game was a time scramble
although, as in Rd3, I had more time than my opponent.} 30. Rxf5+ Ke8 31. Rcf1
Kd8 32. Bd6 Kc8 33. R1f2 Qd1+ 34. Rf1 Qd4+ 35. Qf2 Rg8+ 36. Kh2 Qg4 37. Rf8+
Kb7 38. Rxa8 Rxa8 39. Qg3 {I gave up scoring the moves here but reconstructed
the rset of the game afterwards.} Qxg3+ 40. Kxg3 Rg8+ 41. Kf4 Rg2 42. Ke3 Rxa2
43. Rf5 a5 44. Rxh5 b4 45. Rxh6 b3 46. e6 b2 47. e7 ({Had he played} 47. exd7
b1=Q 48. d8=Q {then I had} Qe4#) 47... b1=Q 48. e8=Q Qe1+ {An exciting finish.
Ralph Mellor was the 1975 winner. I now led with 5/5. A.Graham and N.Harris
had 4.5 I was due to Play Graham, Nick Harris had to play Ralph Mellor.} 0-1

