{Time limit 20 moves per hr} 1. Nf3 d5 2. c4 d4 3. d3 c5 4. g3 Nc6 5. Bg2 e5 6.
Nbd2 f5 7. O-O Nf6 8. Nb3 {Not a very good move. The Knight on d2 leaves the
d-Pawn loose. Maybe Re1, Nf1 then e3} ({correct is} 8. e3 {in this type of
position}) 8... a5 9. Bd2 {[15'] Again this serves no useful purpose.} Be7 {
[4']} 10. Rb1 O-O 11. Bg5 {An admission that his 9th move was wrong.} Qd6 12.
Nbd2 h6 13. Bxf6 Bxf6 14. Qa4 {Another mysterious move. Where is the Queen
going?} Bd7 15. a3 ({Not} 15. Qb5 $4 Nb4) 15... Kh8 16. Qc2 g5 17. e3 $2 {
Losing the game. White decides to try to break out but he simply opens up a
position in which Black's pieces are all better placed than White's.} dxe3 18.
fxe3 g4 19. Ne1 Bg5 20. e4 {Seemingly the only move worth trying but Black's
f-Pawn marches on.} f4 21. gxf4 exf4 {White now thought for ages.} 22. e5 {
Hoping to get the Bishop out.} Qd4+ 23. Kh1 Qxe5 {This seemed more solid than..
.....} ({....} 23... f3 24. Ndxf3 {etc.}) 24. Bd5 Nd4 25. Qc3 {The pin on the
Knight doesn't help much.} Qe2 26. Nc2 g3 27. Bg2 Bc6 28. Rg1 Qh5 29. Nf1 f3 {
Fairly 'night-mare-ish' for White} 30. Nxd4 fxg2+ 31. Rxg2 Bxg2+ 32. Kxg2 Rf2+
33. Kg1 Bf6 ({Not} 33... Qh3 $4 {with "unstopable" mate in view of} 34. Nf5+ {
and Black gets mated! A fairly crushing win. It gets quite claustrophobic
viewed from White's side.}) 0-1

