{1hr 30' each/game} 1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 c5 3. d5 b5 4. a4 $6 {As in Ward - Kinsman
game 49 in Pinski's book. It is given as "dangerous for White".} bxc4 5. Nc3 d6
6. e4 Ba6 7. Nf3 $6 {Ward played 7.f4 which Pinski suggests is better than 7.
Nf3} g6 ({Pinski gives} 7... Nbd7 8. Nd2 ({However} 8. a5 Z0 {intending} 9. Qa4
) 8... Ne5 {as better for Black.}) 8. Nd2 Bg7 9. Bxc4 ({I was expecting} 9.
Nxc4) 9... Bxc4 10. Nxc4 Nbd7 11. O-O O-O 12. f4 {[23']} Nb6 {[9'] He was
already getting behind on time.} 13. Na5 $6 {This came as a surprise.} Nbxd5
14. Nxd5 ({Clearly} 14. Nc6 Nxc3 {is no good.}) 14... Qxa5 15. Nxe7+ Kh8 16. e5
{The best move.} ({If} 16. Qxd6 Nxe4 {is good for Black.}) 16... dxe5 17. fxe5
Ng8 18. Nxg8 {[62'] After a long think.} Kxg8 {[25']} 19. Bg5 {Again the best.}
Bxe5 {This seemed risky but I couldn't find a better line.} 20. Be7 Bd4+ 21.
Kh1 Rfe8 22. Qf3 f5 23. Qd5+ Kh8 24. Rad1 {Threatening 25.Rxd4} Qxa4 25. b3 Qa6
{I was unsure whether this was better than 25.......Qb4. From a6 the Queen
covers f6 should White play Rxd4.White was now very short of time.} 26. Rf4 $2
{Having to rush he makes a mistake.} Qe2 27. Rfxd4 ({If} 27. Bh4 g5) 27... Qxe7
{With only seconds left the only move is 28.Rf4 to answer 28......Qe1+ with 29.
Rf1} (27... Qxe7 {[0 - 1] However} 28. Rf4 Rad8 29. Qf3 Rxd1+ 30. Qxd1 Qe1+ 31.
Rf1 Qxd1 32. Rxd1 Rb8 33. Rd3 {or 33.Rb1} c4 {wins}) 0-1

