{30' each/game} 1. e4 d5 2. exd5 Nf6 3. Bc4 Nxd5 4. Nf3 Nc6 5. h3 Bf5 6. O-O e6
7. d4 Be7 8. Bb5 Bf6 9. c3 O-O 10. Re1 a6 11. Ba4 b5 12. Bb3 {Possibly 12.Bc2
is better.} Na5 13. Nbd2 Nxb3 14. Nxb3 c6 15. Nc5 Re8 16. Bd2 Be7 17. b4 Bxc5
18. bxc5 f6 19. g4 Bg6 20. Nh4 Bf7 {The Knight proves to be a better piece
than the Bishop. So maybe leaving the Bishop on g6 is better.} 21. f4 Qc7 22.
Qf3 g6 (22... e5 23. Nf5 ({Therefore} 23. fxe5 fxe5 24. Nf5) 23... exf4 24. Nd6
Rxe1+ 25. Rxe1 g5 {is O.K. for Black}) 23. Re2 a5 {Black was getting behind on
time and seems to be playing "safe" moves quickly.} 24. a3 f5 $5 {This isn't
"safe" leaving a big hole on e5.} 25. g5 Qd8 26. Qf2 Kg7 (26... Qc7 $142 {
which prevents 27.Nf3}) 27. Nf3 h6 28. gxh6+ {I had to decide betwee 28.gxh6+
and 28.h4. The latter leads to an open h-file and almost certain Rook
exchanges. His weakmess on c6 might tie him down. However, I felt that I had
to play quickly and decided that I could maybe get pressure down the g-file.}
Kxh6 29. Ne5 Kg7 $2 {Short of time and concentratinggetting his Rooks to the
h-file he overlooks the c-pawn.} 30. Nxc6 Qf6 31. Qg3 Rh8 32. Ne5 Rh6 33. Rh2
Rah8 34. Kf2 Rh4 35. Rg1 a4 36. Qf3 Be8 {He was now very short of time and
resorts to shuffling his pieces about.} 37. Rg3 Bf7 38. Rhg2 R4h6 39. Rh2 Be8
40. Kg2 Bf7 41. Qe2 Be8 42. c4 bxc4 43. Qxc4 {Black lost on time.} 1-0

