{2hr each/game} 1. e4 e6 2. d4 d5 3. Nd2 c5 4. exd5 exd5 5. Bb5+ Nc6 6. Ngf3
Bd6 7. dxc5 Bxc5 8. O-O Nge7 9. Nb3 Bd6 10. h3 O-O 11. Re1 Bf5 12. Nbd4 Bg6 13.
Bd3 a6 14. Bg5 Qc7 15. Nxc6 Nxc6 (15... bxc6 {Supports the d-Pawn but isolates
the a-Pawn. I felt that 15......Nxc6 was better, even though it is riskier.})
16. Bxg6 {He played this almost instantly and offered a draw. I think that he
may have been playing for a draw. (Why? he is White and the top graded player
in the major)} (16. Bxg6 {He might have at least played on to see if I could
avoid losing a Pawn e.g.} hxg6 17. Qxd5 {I was unsure about this which is why
I agreed a draw. Black must continue} Nb4 18. Qd2 f6 $1 (18... Nxc2 {is bad})
19. Be3 Rad8 {(threatening 20.....Bh2+) and regains the Pawn?} 20. Nd4 {
may hang on to it- so I was probably right to accept the draw.}) 1/2-1/2

