{30' each/game} 1. d4 Nf6 2. Nf3 g6 3. Bf4 Bg7 4. e3 O-O 5. Bd3 d6 6. h3 Nbd7
7. c3 b6 8. O-O Bb7 9. Re1 c5 10. Nbd2 Re8 11. e4 e5 12. dxe5 Nxe5 13. Bc2 ({
Of course not} 13. Nxe5 $4 {which loses a piece.}) 13... Nh5 14. Bh2 Qd7 15.
Nxe5 dxe5 16. Nf3 Qc7 17. Qe2 a5 18. Rad1 h6 19. Bb3 Kh7 20. Nh4 Nf6 21. Qc4
Re7 22. f3 $2 {I now had one of those nice moments when I suddenly realised I
couls win a piece!} Ba6 23. Qa4 {White, who had played 22.f3? quickly and
confidently, since my last move had been a defensive one, suddenly finds his
Queen short of squares.} b5 24. Qa3 b4 25. Qa4 c4 26. Bc2 Qc5+ {This qins a
piece, but..........} ({...........} 26... Qb6+ {wins the Queen! A problem
with rapid play games is if one sees a winning move one doesn't spend time
looking for a better one.}) 27. Kh1 Bb5 28. cxb4 Bxa4 29. bxc5 Bxc2 30. Rc1 Bd3
31. b3 cxb3 32. axb3 Ne8 {We both had to move fairly quickly now.} 33. Ra1 Rc7
34. b4 Rca7 35. Red1 Bb5 36. Rdb1 axb4 37. Rxa7 Rxa7 38. Rxb4 Bd7 39. Bg1 Bf6
$2 {Moving quickly. I saw the problem as soon as I let go of the Bishop.} 40.
Nxg6 $2 {White "half spots" the chance. However, worried that his Knight is
en-prise he doesn't play 40.c6!} (40. c6 $1 {After which I have to play} Bxc6
41. Bxa7 Bxh4 {when the win is problematical (although likely as White was
nearly out of time)}) 40... Rc7 {All this was played very quickly} 41. Nf8+ Kg7
42. Nxd7 Rxd7 43. c6 Rc7 44. Rc4 Nd6 45. Rc1 Nb5 46. Bb6 Rc8 47. Bc5 {Setting
a little trap- always a good idea in a time rush.} Bd8 48. Kh2 Kg6 49. Rb1 Nd4
50. Bxd4 exd4 51. Rc1 Kf6 52. Kg3 Ke6 53. Kf2 Kd6 54. Rd1 Bb6 ({I was
momentarily tempted to play} 54... Kxc6 {which leaves the d-Pawn immune.}) 55.
Ke2 Kxc6 56. Kd3 {White lost on time.} 0-1

