{1hr 45' each all moves} 1. e4 e5 2. Bc4 Nf6 3. d4 {Move 3 and I didn't know
what to do!} Nxe4 4. dxe5 Qe7 5. Qe2 Nc5 6. Nf3 Ne6 7. Nc3 Nc6 8. Nd5 Qd8 {
By this stage I felt that it had all gone wrong. My opponent, who is unsighted
and using a braille set, seemed quite confident.} 9. c3 d6 (9... b6 $142 10.
O-O Be7) 10. Nf4 {Letting me slightly off the hook.} (10. Bg5 $142 Be7 11. Nxe7
Nxe7 12. O-O-O) 10... Nxf4 (10... dxe5 $142 11. Nxe5 Nxe5 12. Qxe5 Bd6 13. Nxe6
Bxe5 14. Nxd8 Kxd8 15. O-O Bf5) 11. Bxf4 d5 ({Safer is} 11... Be7 12. O-O-O O-O
) 12. Rd1 Be6 13. Bb3 ({Fritz prefers} 13. Bxd5 Bxd5 14. Qb5 a6 15. Qxd5 Qxd5
16. Rxd5) 13... Ne7 {White wins a Pawn now but it was worth it to get some
play.} (13... Qd7 $142 14. O-O Be7 15. Rfe1 O-O-O) 14. Qb5+ c6 15. Qxb7 Qc8 16.
Qxc8+ Rxc8 17. h3 Ng6 18. Bg3 ({I had expected} 18. Be3 {which prevents 18.....
.Bc5}) 18... Bc5 19. O-O O-O 20. Nd4 Rfe8 21. Rfe1 Bd7 22. Ba4 Ne7 23. e6 fxe6
24. Nxe6 Bb6 25. Nd4 Kf7 26. Re2 Nf5 $2 {I played badly all day} ({Correct is} 
26... Ng6) 27. Rxe8 Kxe8 28. Nxf5 Bxf5 29. Re1+ $2 (29. Rxd5 {and he is a good
2 Pawns up.}) 29... Kf7 30. Bh4 Bc5 {I offered a draw here.} 31. Bg5 Bd7 32.
Kf1 Rb8 33. b4 Bb6 $2 {Ludicrous. After all my efforts. I now played this move
almost instantaneously and, as usual, immediately realised my error.} 34. Re7+
Kg6 35. Rxd7 Kxg5 36. Bxc6 {I could resign here. However he was getting short
of time (as was I) so I played on, without much hope.} Rc8 {From now on I
played very quickly in the hope that his time shortage might give me a chance
to pick up a Pawn (or 2)} 37. Rxd5+ Kf6 38. b5 a6 {completely wrong in view of
39.Bd7. However, playing fast he doesn't spot this.} ({Fritz gives} 38... Rd8 {
as best. Clearly, in real life, I must keep the Rook on to have any chance.})
39. a4 (39. Bd7) 39... axb5 40. axb5 Ba5 41. Rd3 Rc7 42. c4 Re7 {Threatening
mate, no less!} 43. Rf3+ Kg5 44. Re3 Rf7 45. f3 Rf4 46. Re5+ Kh6 47. Re4 {
In view of his now severe time shortage. White should concentrate on pushing
his QS Pawns} Rf8 48. Bd5 Rc8 49. Re6+ g6 50. Ke2 Rb8 51. Ke3 Bb6+ 52. Ke4 Bc5
53. g4 {He now launches into another attempt to embarass my King, rather than
mobilizing his QS. This all uses up more of his time.} Kg5 54. Rc6 Bb4 55. Rc7
Kh6 56. h4 Re8+ ({Threatening} 56... Z0 57. g5+ Kh5 58. Rxh7#) 57. Kd4 Rh8 58.
c5 g5 59. h5 Rd8 60. Rc6+ Kg7 61. Kc4 Ba3 62. h6+ Kh8 {This was all happening
very fast. The possibility of stalemate now comes into the equation.} 63. Rd6
Rc8 64. Rc6 Rf8 65. Rc7 {He is still trying for an attack instead of pushing
his Pawns.} Bb2 66. Be4 Rd8 67. Rxh7+ Kg8 68. Re7 Bf6 69. Rc7 Be5 70. h7+ Kh8 {
Now if I can lose my Bishop I have a draw.} 71. Re7 Bd6 {We were now moving
like lightning. I am trying to give my Bishop away, but this is no good. ( see
variation) However he only sees that his Rook is attacked and moves it.} 72.
Re6 (72. cxd6 Rc8+ 73. Rc7 {Wins for him}) 72... Bxc5 {Played in the rush and
failing to notice that I am no longer stalemated. However it doesn't matter as
he has no time left. White lost on time.} 0-1

