{30' each/game} 1. e4 e5 2. d4 exd4 3. Qxd4 Nc6 4. Qe3 {He plays this move
quite regularly and had done so in the past against me in friendly games.} b6 {
Since he was familiar with this line I try to take him out of the main lines}
5. Nc3 Bb7 6. Bd2 Bc5 7. Qg3 g6 {I was tempted to play 7.......Nf6 but didn't
want to spend time thinking about it, so played the text quickly.} ({However} 
7... Nf6 {is best, and if} 8. Qxg7 Rg8 9. Qh6 Bxf2+) 8. Bc4 Bd6 (8... Nf6 {
is still on.}) 9. Bf4 {I had only briefly considered this but now spent 5'
trying to find a good move} Bb4 (9... Bxf4 10. Qxf4 {Threatens mate and 10.....
..Qe7 leves the c-Pawn hanging}) (9... Qe7 10. Bxd6 Qxd6 11. Qxd6 cxd6 {
leaves isolated, doubled, d-Pawns and} 12. Nd5 {is a problem.}) 10. O-O-O ({
Now after} 10. Bxc7 Qf6 {I may be able to double his c-Pawn} (10... Qe7 {Fritz}
)) 10... Bxc3 (10... Nf6 11. Bxc7 Nh5 12. Bxd8 Nxg3 {Fritz}) 11. Qxc3 Qf6 12.
Qxf6 (12. Qg3 {Fritz}) 12... Nxf6 13. Bd5 {Rather an odd move} (13. Bxc7 Rc8
14. Bd6 Nxe4) 13... d6 14. f3 (14. Ne2 $142) 14... Nxd5 15. exd5 {Now he loses
a pawn.} (15. Rxd5 $142) 15... Nb4 16. Re1+ $2 (16. c4 $142) 16... Kd7 17. c4
$2 {Possibly an example of what often happens in these quickplay games. A
player rejects a move. Thinks about other moves and can't find a good one.
Suddenly realising that time is getting on and a move is needed, the player
plays the move originally rejected.} Nd3+ 18. Kd2 Nxf4 19. Re4 Nh5 20. Nh3 h6
21. Rhe1 Rae8 22. Nf4 Rxe4 23. Rxe4 Nxf4 24. Rxf4 f5 25. b4 c6 26. Rd4 cxd5 27.
cxd5 Re8 28. Kd3 Re1 0-1

