{Time control: All moves 1 hour 30 minutes.} 1. c4 Nf6 2. Nc3 g6 3. d4 Bg7 4.
e4 d6 5. f4 {King's Indian Defence, Four Pawns Attack} O-O 6. Nf3 c5 7. d5 e6
8. Be2 exd5 9. exd5 {Dick's usual choice over many years. This doesn't offer
White any advantage, but is very solid and minimises all risks.} (9. cxd5 {
Is the main line, where we have reached a Benoni position that is highly
theroretical and can become tactically dangerous for both sides. For example}
Re8 10. e5 dxe5 11. fxe5 Ng4 12. e6 fxe6 13. Bg5 Qb6 14. Qd2 c4 15. O-O-O exd5
16. Rhe1 Nc6 17. Bxc4 {With a complex but balanced game.}) 9... Re8 10. O-O Bf5
11. Bd3 {This was all played in an earlier game between us, where I found it
extremely difficult to unblance the position and create any winning chances.}
Qd7 ({Previouly I played} 11... Ne4 12. Nxe4 Bxe4 13. Bxe4 Rxe4 14. Qc2 Qe7 15.
Bd2 Nd7 16. Rae1 Re8 {with inevitable simplifications.} 17. Bc3 $6 ({Better was
} 17. Rxe4 Qxe4 18. Qxe4 Rxe4 19. b3) 17... Bxc3 18. Qxc3 Qf6 $15 {With a
small edge for Black.}) 12. Qc2 Bxd3 13. Qxd3 Na6 14. a3 Nc7 15. Bd2 {Dick had
played his first fifteen moves very quickly, but now had to take stock after
Black's surprise} b5 $5 {After our earlier game I had looked for a way to
generate play and unbalance the position and had come across this interesting
pawn sacrifice in Peter Williams (2196) 0-1 Mark Hebden (2506) Guernsey 2011.
When I first saw the sacrifice I was sceptical and thought Mark Hebden was
taking an inferior position to just create some winning chances against a
lower rated player. But taking a closer look, I realised it's a very good idea.
} 16. Nxb5 $6 ({Williams played} 16. b3 Rab8 17. Rae1 bxc4 18. bxc4 Rb3 {
And Black is already asking questions.}) ({More recently, in Vincent Colin 
(2412) 0 - 1 Mark Hebden (2548) from Guernsey 2013} 16. cxb5 Reb8 17. b4 cxb4
18. axb4 Nxb5 19. Ra5 Nxc3 20. Bxc3 {With an equal position}) 16... Nxb5 17.
cxb5 Reb8 $6 {[Glenn thought for 10' on this (RAC)] My first tough decision of
the game and I fail to find the correct solution. I wanted both rooks on the
queenside to exert as much pressure there as possible, and liked the idea of
using the vacated e8 square for ... Nf6-e8-c7 and capturing on b5, while
moving the knight then opens up Bg7 for further pressure against b2.} (17...
Ne4 {I quite liked this move, opening up the position for Bg7 and maintaining
Re8 on the open file.} 18. Rae1 (18. Bc3 $6 c4 19. Qxc4 Rac8 20. Qb3 Nxc3 21.
bxc3 Rxc3 {Is good for Black.}) (18. Rab1 $2 Nxd2 19. Nxd2 Re3 $1 {A nice move
to double the rooks.} 20. Qc4 Rae8 $19 {And White has serious problems with
Black's dominating pieces.}) 18... Nxd2 19. Nxd2 Bxb2 20. a4 Bd4+ 21. Kh1 $15 {
May be Black has a tiny initiative, but was I afraid of the exchanges along
the e-line?}) ({With my other alternative} 17... Rab8 18. a4 Qb7 ({I wanted to
play} 18... a6 $6 19. bxa6 Rxb2 {when I wasn't sure how to capture the
potentially dangerous a6 pawn. For example} 20. Bc3 Rb3 21. Rfb1 Reb8 $2 22.
Nd2 $18 Rxb1+ 23. Rxb1 Rxb1+ 24. Qxb1 Qxa4 25. Qb2 {and Black is lost.}) 19.
Rad1 {I had not seen the rook lift} Re4 $15 (19... Qxd5 20. Qxd5 Nxd5 21. Bc1
Nb6 22. b3 {offers mutual chances.})) (17... Qb7 18. Rad1 Rab8 (18... Qxd5 19.
Bc3 {was the line Dick expected.}) 19. a4 Re4 $15 {Again this rook lift gives
Black an initiative.}) ({Best is} 17... Qc7 18. b3 Qb6 {[RAC]}) 18. Bc3 $2 {
After this White has a poor game.} (18. a4 $142 a6 19. Bc3 Qa7 (19... axb5 20.
axb5 Rxa1 21. Rxa1 Nh5 ({Trying to recover the pawn immediately is suicide.} 
21... Rxb5 $4 22. Ra8+ Ne8 23. Qe3 $18 Kf8 24. Rxe8+ Qxe8 25. Bxg7+) (21... Qb7
22. Ra6 Qxd5 23. Qxd5 Nxd5 24. Bxg7 Kxg7 25. Rxd6 Ne3 26. Rd3 $14) 22. g3 Bxc3
23. bxc3 Rxb5 24. Ra8+ Kg7 {With a level game.}) 20. Qc4 Ng4 21. Rae1 axb5 22.
axb5 Qa4 23. Qxa4 Rxa4 24. f5 {With mutual chances.}) 18... Qxb5 19. Qxb5 Rxb5
20. Rad1 Ng4 {Again, Black has a position with several decent moves. My main
concern was White transferring his knight Nf3-d2-c4 where it could attack the
weak d6 pawn, control b3 and defend White's b2 pawn.} (20... Re8 {Try to use
the e-line}) (20... Rab8 {Increase the pressure agaisnt b2.}) (20... Ne4 {
Anchor the knight with ...f7-f5}) (20... c4 {Threaten to capture the d5 pawn})
21. Bxg7 $2 {After this, White's pawn weaknesses are probably too much. 
[Desperation (RAC)]} (21. a4 $142 Rbb8 22. Rfe1 Re8 23. Ng5 Bxc3 24. bxc3 Ne3
25. Rd2 Re7 $17 26. Rde2 Rae8 27. c4 h6 28. Nf3 Nxd5 29. Rxe7 Nxe7 {White can
fight on a pawn down.}) 21... Kxg7 22. Rd2 Rab8 23. f5 $2 (23. b4 $142 {
trying to exchange off some pawns.} cxb4 24. axb4 Rxb4 25. h3 Nf6 26. f5) 23...
Rxb2 24. fxg6 hxg6 25. Re1 Ne5 {White resigns. There may have been stronger
moves, but the simplification into the rook ending was the clearest to me.} ({
For example} 25... Nf6 26. Re7 c4 27. Rxb2 (27. Rc7 c3 28. Rxc3 Rb1+ 29. Kf2
Ne4+) 27... Rxb2 28. Ng5 c3 29. Rc7 c2) ({After the game continuation} 25...
Ne5 {I had expected something like} 26. Rxb2 Nxf3+ 27. gxf3 Rxb2 28. Re7 {
With 4 weak isolated pawns White can only counter-attack} (28. Re3 Rd2 {
And another pawn drops.}) 28... Rd2 ({I hadn't considered the more direct} 
28... Kf6 29. Rxa7 Ke5 30. Rxf7 c4 31. Rc7 Kd4 32. a4 c3 33. a5 c2 34. a6 Rb1+
35. Kf2 c1=Q) 29. Rxa7 Rxd5 30. Kf2 Kf6 31. a4 Rd2+ 32. Kg3 Ra2 33. a5 Ke6 {
With an easily won rook ending. White's rook is poorly placed in front of his
passed pawn, Black has advancing connected passed pawns which can be supported
by his king. For example} 34. a6 Kd5 35. Ra8 Kd4 36. a7 d5 37. Kg4 c4 38. Kg5
c3 39. Kf6 c2 40. Rc8 Kd3 41. Rc7 Rxa7 42. Rxa7 c1=Q) 0-1

