{90' each/game} 1. c4 e5 2. Nc3 f5 3. d3 Nf6 4. Bg5 Be7 5. g3 c6 6. Bg2 O-O 7.
Nf3 d6 8. O-O Na6 9. a3 h6 10. Bxf6 (10. Bd2 $142) 10... Bxf6 11. Rc1 Nc7 12.
b4 a6 13. Qb3 Be6 14. Qb1 g5 15. b5 $2 {Simply losing a Pawn and giving me
tactical chances.} axb5 16. cxb5 {He played this automatically. 16.Nd2 is
better.} Rxa3 17. Nd2 {Worried about 17.......e4 attacking both Knights.} ({
After} 17. bxc6 e4 18. dxe4 Bxc3 19. cxb7 {However a human player is unlikely
to do this.}) 17... e4 18. Qb4 (18. Ncxe4 {is given by Fritz as best. However,
a human player isn't likely to play that either. Now he loses another Pawn.})
18... Rxc3 19. Rxc3 (19. bxc6 {preffered by Fritz again. A human player
wouldnot go for this.}) 19... Bxc3 $2 {Played with minimal consideration and
overlooking..............} ({...................} 19... Nd5) 20. Qxc3 Nxb5 21.
Qb2 exd3 22. exd3 Qf6 23. Qb1 Ra8 24. Nc4 Qd4 25. Re1 Kf7 {Not really
necessary as the Bishop can't be taken in view of .......Ra1. However, 2 Pawns
up I just play it solid.} 26. Qd1 g4 ({I suspected that} 26... Ra2 {was the
move but I preffered not to give him any chances.}) 27. Bf1 $2 (27. Qb1 $142)
27... Ra2 28. Ne3 h5 29. Qb1 f4 30. Nc2 Qb2 31. Qxb2 Rxb2 32. Rc1 f3 33. d4 Nc3
34. Bd3 {This loses quickly. 34.Ne1 hangs on a bit longer.} Bf5 35. Ne1 {
Loses a piece} (35. Bxf5 {Loses the exchange to} Ne2+ 36. Kf1 Nxc1) 35... Bxd3
36. Nxd3 Ne2+ 37. Kf1 Rd2 0-1

